{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1,330p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\n     2\t\n     3\t> [!WARNING]  \n     4\t> **Status: Proposed / Deferred.** The Semantic VM's linear CESK state is theoretically sound as an architecture-agnostic continuation format, but true heterogeneous network migration requires addressing several critical defects identified in the 2026-09-14 architectural review. This revision (r2) answers that review's findings 1–19; §7.11 lists the acceptance blockers that keep this warning standing until an implementation phase closes each of them with tests.\n     5\t\n     6\tThe Semantic VM's linear CESK state (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) is proposed as the canonical exchange format for network-transparent continuations. Because this lowered representation resolves execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST, it provides a simpler target for specialized execution engines (e.g., WebAssembly, LLVM, hardware FPGA) to participate in the `datom.world` ecosystem.\n     7\t\n     8\tTo safely \"lift on park\" and \"lower on resume\" across heterogeneous boundaries without violating host isolation or concurrency invariants, the following contracts must be fully specified before this feature is accepted:\n     9\t\n    10\t1. **Safepoint Reconstruction Metadata:** `:yin.code/source` mappings are insufficient for state reconstruction. The safepoint set must be exactly the machine's parking transitions — including effect-producing `:call`s — and reconstruction metadata must separate what is derivable from the canonical code alone (stack *effects*, lexical *requirements*) from what is per-activation state only the frame can carry (absolute depths, captured environments). Foreign engines publish per-segment safepoint maps derived from the canonical stream so arbitrary hardware states can be predictably lifted into the canonical CESK format.\n    11\t2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive encoding over a **disjoint tagged grammar** is required — one a program's own map literals cannot counterfeit — to encode these local references into portable descriptors, together with per-primitive **semantic profiles** (a name recovers a binding, not a meaning) and explicit, total failure modes for un-serializable resources. Cursor aliasing is observable and must survive the round trip.\n    12\t3. **Dependency Closure & Context:** A parked fragment is not a complete configuration. The format must carry its complete scheduler context — required primitives *with profiles*, loaded modules, referenced parked records, the fresh-name counter, and the reachable store slice — discovered by a conservative fixed point that includes explicit store operands, and must restore into an **isolated execution store** so the receiver's own state cannot change resolution.\n    13\t4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. Exclusion requires a **grounded authority** — a resource the grantor's boundary actually possesses, named in the value, independent of carriers — plus an **exporting state** that fences the source before publication, and **effect fencing** with authority epochs and stable operation ids, because absence of a lapse record is never evidence of tenure.\n    14\t5. **Code Identity (Content Addressing):** Because tempids (`:segment 123`) are local to a single machine's database, continuations must refer to code via immutable, versioned semantic profiles — a **canonical instruction vector** (positional tuples, not EAV) whose address is computed over the loader's resolved interpretation, whose addressed payload is the vector itself, and whose interpretation is pinned by a contract stamp versioning the *complete* execution contract.\n    15\t\n    16\t---\n    17\t\n    18\t# Universal Continuation Format — protocol draft\n    19\t\n    20\tStatus: Phase 5 design draft, 2026-09-14; revised 2026-09-14 (r2) per the\n    21\tarchitectural review `collab/1789387292000-architect-ucf-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`;\n    22\tamended 2026-09-14 (r3) by owner ruling — §7.3's canonical form is the\n    23\tpositional instruction tuple vector, not EAV (§7.3.2).\n    24\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md); builds on the Phase 0\n    25\tcontract in [`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md) (§1–§4), the storage\n    26\tcontract in [`dao.jing.md`](./dao.jing.md), the stream contract in\n    27\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), and the lease vocabulary in\n    28\t[`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md). The five blockers above are answered in\n    29\t§7.3–§7.7, one section each, in dependency order: code identity first, because\n    30\tevery other part names code.\n    31\t\n    32\tEvery sentence in §7.2–§7.9 is a rule of the proposed protocol. §7.11 records\n    33\twhat is deliberately left open, as acceptance blockers. Nothing below is\n    34\timplemented; the warning at the top stands until an implementation phase\n    35\tresolves each blocker with tests.\n    36\t\n    37\t## §7.1 Stance: a continuation is a value, not a session\n    38\t\n    39\tThe semantic VM already parks as pure data (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.5): a\n    40\twait entry or a `:parked-continuation` record is `{segment pc env stack k}`\n    41\tplus a reason and resource ids, with no handle and no closure attached. That\n    42\trecord is *locally* complete and *globally* meaningless: its segment is a\n    43\ttempid, its stream ids are keys into this VM's store, its environment may hold\n    44\thost functions, and nothing says which primitives it assumed.\n    45\t\n    46\tThe Universal Continuation Format (UCF) is the **lift** of that local record\n    47\tinto a self-describing value that any conforming engine can **lower** back\n    48\tinto its own registers, and the discipline that governs who may lower it.\n    49\tThree roles participate, and they are stream roles, not parties to a call:\n    50\t\n    51\t| Role | Does | Holds |\n    52\t|---|---|---|\n    53\t| **Emitter** | lifts a parked configuration into a UCF value and appends it to a medium | nothing, once appended |\n    54\t| **Carrier** | any stream, file, DHT, or socket that moves the value; sees opaque data | nothing |\n    55\t| **Resumer** | reads the value, satisfies its declared context, obtains custody, lowers, runs | custody, for the duration of a lease |\n    56\t\n    57\tThree consequences are load-bearing. First, **a UCF value is content**: it has\n    58\ta content address under `dao.jing`, equal values converge on one address, and\n    59\tcarrying it twice is harmless (`dao.jing.md`, *Materialization rule*). Second,\n    60\t**a UCF value on a stream is not a claim**: a stream has no privileged reader\n    61\t(`datom.world.md`, *Streams*), so emission transfers nothing. Custody is\n    62\tarbitrated above the stream, by leases over datoms (§7.7), exactly where\n    63\t`dao.stream.md` *Explicitly Absent* says a take belongs. Third, **content is\n    64\tnot occurrence**: the same parked computation can be encoded more than once\n    65\t(sharing thresholds differ, §7.5.3), so the encodings address differently,\n    66\twhile remaining one *checkpoint occurrence*. Content identity is for\n    67\tdeduplication and materialization; occurrence identity is what custody names\n    68\t(§7.3.4, §7.7). Conflating them was a defect of the first draft; this draft\n    69\tnever does.\n    70\t\n    71\tThe reference engine for every rule below is `yin.vm.semantic`. Its\n    72\tconfiguration ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ with C = ⟨seg, pc, val, St⟩ (`yin.vm.semantic.md`\n    73\t§4.1) is the **canonical CESK**. A foreign engine conforms by lifting into and\n    74\tlowering from this configuration, never by extending it.\n    75\t\n    76\t## §7.2 Vocabulary and envelope\n    77\t\n    78\tUCF facts live under `:yin.k/*` (K for the continuation component of CESK).\n    79\tA UCF value is one open map dispatching on `:yin.k/type`; a consumer ignores\n    80\tqualified keys it does not understand, as for every DaoStream envelope. The\n    81\tvalue must survive the host codec structurally unchanged: no function, host\n    82\tobject, or live handle anywhere inside it (`dao.stream.md`, *Envelopes*).\n    83\tEvery value it contains is encoded in the disjoint tagged grammar of §7.5 —\n    84\tnever a bare marker map.\n    85\t\n    86\t```clojure\n    87\t{:yin.k/type        :yin.k/continuation\n    88\t :yin.k/contract    {:yin.code/contract \"v2\" :yin.k/version 0}   ; §7.3.3\n    89\t :yin.k/id          :segment/sha256-…          ; §7.3.4  content address of this map minus :yin.k/id\n    90\t :yin.k/occurrence  :yin.k/o-…                  ; §7.7    the checkpoint occurrence — the lease subject\n    91\t :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/o-…                        ; predecessor, or absent on a first park\n    92\t                    :dao.lease/lease   :dao.lease/l-…                     ; the grant it ran under\n    93\t                    :yin.k/emitter     <attribution>}\n    94\t :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}     ; §7.7.3  the authority medium, named in the value\n    95\t :yin.k/frame       {…}                        ; §7.4  the canonical CESK at a safepoint\n    96\t :yin.k/values      {…}                        ; §7.5.3  shared subvalues, by content address\n    97\t :yin.k/cells       {…}                        ; §7.5.3  cursor cells: logical identity + position\n    98\t :yin.k/scheduler   {…}                        ; §7.6.3  fresh-name state and referenced parked records\n    99\t :yin.k/requires    {…}                        ; §7.6  dependency closure, with discovery status\n   100\t :yin.k/store       {…}                        ; §7.6.2  the store slice restored as an isolated store\n   101\t :yin.k/carried     [ … ]                      ; §7.3.4  canonical instruction vectors shipped inline\n   102\t :yin.k/policy      :yin.k/exclusive}          ; §7.7  or :yin.k/fork\n   103\t```\n   104\t\n   105\tThe parts answer four questions a resumer must be able to ask of the data\n   106\talone: *what code* (`:yin.k/requires`, `:yin.k/carried`, and every segment\n   107\taddress inside the frame), *what state* (`:yin.k/frame`, `:yin.k/values`,\n   108\t`:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/store`, `:yin.k/scheduler`), *what world*\n   109\t(`:yin.k/requires`, `:yin.k/arbitration`), and *who may run it*\n   110\t(`:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, with the custody facts of §7.7 on\n   111\ttheir own medium).\n   112\t\n   113\tA halted computation travels as the sibling value:\n   114\t\n   115\t```clojure\n   116\t{:yin.k/type      :yin.k/result\n   117\t :yin.k/contract  {…}\n   118\t :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/o-…     ; the occurrence that halted\n   119\t :yin.k/value     <encoded>}      ; the final accumulator, in the §7.5 grammar\n   120\t```\n   121\t\n   122\tBoth envelope kinds take their identity by the same rule: `:yin.k/id` is the\n   123\t`dao.jing` segment address of the map with `:yin.k/id` removed (§7.3.4).\n   124\t\n   125\tOutcomes of every UCF operation are data: one map dispatching on\n   126\t`:yin.k/status`, in the DaoStream result convention. Where an underlying\n   127\tstream outcome is involved, it is preserved unchanged under its own dispatch\n   128\tkey `:dao.stream/outcome` — UCF never renames or nests it under a private\n   129\tkey. The full algebra is in §7.9.\n   130\t\n   131\t## §7.3 Code identity — content-addressed semantic profiles (blocker 5)\n   132\t\n   133\t### 7.3.1 The problem restated as data\n   134\t\n   135\t`:segment 123` is an entity id minted by one transactor; another machine's\n   136\t`123` is different code or no code. A continuation naming code by tempid is\n   137\ttherefore not a value, it is a pointer. UCF names code by **what it is**.\n   138\t\n   139\t### 7.3.2 The canonical instruction vector and its address\n   140\t\n   141\tThe segment attribute `:yin.code/hash`, reserved in `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n   142\t§2.2, becomes required on any segment a continuation may reference. Its\n   143\tvalue is the `dao.jing` segment address of the segment's **canonical\n   144\tinstruction vector**. The canonical form has had two predecessors, each\n   145\tnamed so no later plan reinvents it: the first draft's sorted `[e a v]`\n   146\ttriples, which were neither total nor execution-faithful; and the r2\n   147\trevision's attributed-map record (`{pc → {attr → value}}`), which was sound\n   148\tbut carried datom vocabulary into the identity layer — attribute names and\n   149\tadmissibility rules where position and the opcode table suffice. The r2\n   150\tform is superseded by two owner rulings: tuples are the correct way to\n   151\trepresent a semantic AST, though the datom `[e a v t m]` need not be the\n   152\ttuple shape when content-hashing code; and `dao.space.query` assumes no\n   153\tEAV — its `relation` is an arbitrary mixed-dimensional tuple collection\n   154\t(`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:153-158`) — so Datalog-over-code does not\n   155\trequire `[e a v t m]` and UCF must not assume it anywhere for code. The\n   156\tcanonical form is therefore the **positional instruction tuple vector**:\n   157\tone tuple per instruction, pc is the index, and the §2.4 opcode table fixes\n   158\teach mnemonic's tuple arity and operand kinds. Nothing else names a slot.\n   159\t\n   160\t**Input.** The vector is computed from a well-formed batch (§2.6) by first\n   161\tapplying the loader's *resolved interpretation* — the same\n   162\t`index-batch` collapse the loader and the validator perform, where a repeated\n   163\tsingle-valued attribute keeps its **last** value. This is the load-bearing\n   164\tchoice. Two batches whose constants arrive as `1, 2` versus `2, 1` resolve to\n   165\tindexed values `2` and `1`; they *execute differently*, and over the resolved\n   166\tinterpretation they canonicalize differently and address differently. Over\n   167\traw triples they sorted identically — the collision the review demonstrated.\n   168\tCanonicalization and execution now read the same value by construction; there\n   169\tis nothing left to collide. The last-value-wins rule itself is part of the\n   170\tcontract stamp (§7.3.3) and is versioned with it.\n   171\t\n   172\t**The vector.** With the resolution applied, the canonical instruction\n   173\tvector of a segment is (the §2.7 worked segment, excerpted):\n   174\t\n   175\t```clojure\n   176\t[[:closure [x] 6]           ; mnemonic, params, resolved body pc\n   177\t [:push]\n   178\t [:const 10]                ; §2.5 literal: data only\n   179\t [:call 1 false]            ; argc, tail?\n   180\t …]\n   181\t```\n   182\t\n   183\tRules, each replacing heavier machinery from the attributed-map record:\n   184\t\n   185\t- **pc is the index.** No entity ids and no attribute names appear anywhere\n   186\t  in the form: position fixes meaning, the §2.4 table fixes shape, and a\n   187\t  query over code is a query over tuples of the vector, not over EAV.\n   188\t- **Saturation.** Defaults the loader applies are materialized in the tuple —\n   189\t  `:gensym` absent prefix becomes `\"id\"`, `:ffi-call` absent argc becomes\n   190\t  `0`, `:stream-make` absent buffer becomes the default capacity — so a batch\n   191\t  that omitted a defaulted operand and one that stated it canonicalize\n   192\t  identically, because they execute identically.\n   193\t- **Refs are resolved pcs**, exactly as the loader resolves them.\n   194\t- **The header folds away.** `:yin.code/length` is `(count vector)`,\n   195\t  `:yin.code/type :segment` is implied by the form. Provenance\n   196\t  (`:yin.code/derived-from`, every `:yin.code/source`), entity ids, batch\n   197\t  order, `t`, `m`, and `:yin.code/hash` itself are excluded — the address is\n   198\t  computed *over* the vector, which therefore cannot contain it without\n   199\t  hashing the hash being verified.\n   200\t- **Admissible slots.** Each tuple is its mnemonic plus exactly the operands\n   201\t  the §2.4 table gives it, in the table's order. A batch that resolves to\n   202\t  anything else — a mnemonic outside the table, an operand the mnemonic's\n   203\t  tuple has no slot for, a segment-entity attribute beyond the folded and\n   204\t  excluded set — is **non-canonicalizable**, and canonicalization refuses\n   205\t  naming the instruction (the lifted value is never minted; the refusal is\n   206\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :non-canonicalizable`).\n   207\t- **Ordering: none is imposed.** The vector's order is its positions;\n   208\t  canonicalization sorts nothing and compares no operand against another.\n   209\t  Literal operands are values and hash under the same encoder as everything\n   210\t  else, so equal literals address equally however their maps were built.\n   211\t  The first draft's `[:segment …]`-versus-`[0 …]` triple sort — which throws\n   212\t  `Keyword cannot be cast to Number` in the very host it must run on — has\n   213\t  no successor here because there is no sort at all.\n   214\t\n   215\t**The address** is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)` — a\n   216\t`:segment/sha256-<hex>` keyword. The vector *is* the resolved\n   217\tinterpretation, so r2's collision-freedom argument carries over unchanged\n   218\tand needs only restating: canonicalization and execution read the same\n   219\tvalue by construction; there is nothing left to collide. The address fixes\n   220\tthe resolved instruction stream — mnemonics, operands, control graph,\n   221\tsaturations — and nothing else.\n   222\t\n   223\t**What equal addresses do and do not mean.** Two segments address equally\n   224\t**iff** their loader-resolved interpretations are equal; this includes\n   225\tsegments that differ only in entity ids, batch order, provenance, or omitted\n   226\tdefaulted operands. No equivalence beyond that is claimed: different pc\n   227\tlayouts, different `:gensym` prefixes, different but *equivalent* instruction\n   228\tsequences, and compiler-renamed artifacts address **differently**. The\n   229\taddress is a fingerprint of one instruction stream, not a quotient of a\n   230\tsemantics.\n   231\t\n   232\tThe canonical encoding beneath `segment-key` is transitional\n   233\t(`dao.jing.md`, *Open items*). UCF inherits that limit unchanged: addresses\n   234\tare portable exactly as far as `dao.jing` addresses are, and when the pinned\n   235\tbyte encoding lands, `:yin.code/hash` values change with every other minted\n   236\taddress. This is the correct dependency; UCF must not mint a second\n   237\taddressing scheme.\n   238\t\n   239\t### 7.3.3 The contract stamp\n   240\t\n   241\tAn address fixes *which* instructions; it does not fix what `:call` *means*.\n   242\tInterpretation is supplied by the loader and the step loop (§1.1,\n   243\t*Interpretation Creates Semantics*), and the first draft pinned only the\n   244\topcode table — leaving resolution precedence, effect outcomes, call\n   245\trestoration, and scheduling outside the stamp. UCF therefore versions the\n   246\t**complete execution contract**: the stamp names one published revision of\n   247\t\n   248\t- the tuple grammar of §7.3.2 — the mnemonic set, each mnemonic's tuple\n   249\t  arity and operand kinds, and the saturation/defaults table — plus the\n   250\t  §2.6 well-formedness rules and the resolved-interpretation\n   251\t  (last-value-wins) rule;\n   252\t- the §2.4 opcode table and the §4.2 transitions;\n   253\t- the resolution precedence of `resolve-var` (env → store → primitives →\n   254\t  modules);\n   255\t- the effect→outcome mapping of §3.3 and `engine/handle-effect`;\n   256\t- call restoration and the scheduler semantics of §3.5 — wait-entry shapes,\n   257\t  `check-wait-set`'s round order, `semantic-restore`.\n   258\t\n   259\t```clojure\n   260\t:yin.k/contract {:yin.code/contract \"v2\"    ; the revision above, by name\n   261\t                 :yin.k/version    0}       ; this envelope's own version\n   262\t```\n   263\t\n   264\tA resumer whose loader implements a different revision does not guess: it\n   265\treturns `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (§7.9) *before* lowering. Adding an opcode,\n   266\tchanging a transition, or changing the resolution order is a new revision; a\n   267\tresumer on the old one cannot run code that observes the difference, and the\n   268\tstamp is how it finds out before lowering rather than at an unknown integer\n   269\tin `case`. Two revisions may be declared compatible only by a published\n   270\tstatement in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4, never by an engine's own judgment.\n   271\tBecause interpretation is part of identity, an engine's code index is keyed\n   272\t**per stamp revision**: lookup is `(get-in index [stamp address])`, and the\n   273\tsame address under different stamps is a different image the same way the\n   274\tsame datoms under different loaders are a different program.\n   275\t\n   276\t### 7.3.4 References to code inside a continuation\n   277\t\n   278\tEvery place the local record says `:segment <id>` — the control, each\n   279\t`:return` frame, each effect frame, each closure's `:segment` — the UCF frame\n   280\tsays `:yin.k/segment <address>`. Entry pcs and frame pcs are unchanged: a pc\n   281\tis meaningful relative to the resolved stream and is stable under everything\n   282\t§7.3.2 normalizes away.\n   283\t\n   284\t**The addressed object is the vector.** `dao.jing` materializes the exact\n   285\tpayload it is handed and verifies address-equals-hash on every write and read\n   286\t(`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc`, `materialize!`); it supplies no aliases. So the payload\n   287\tstored at a code address **is the canonical instruction vector** — nothing\n   288\telse hashes to that address. A raw segment batch, with tempids and\n   289\tprovenance, materializes under its *own* different address and is not a\n   290\tresolution mechanism for UCF; `:yin.k/carried` carries vectors, not batches.\n   291\t\n   292\tLoading a fetched vector has two paths, and both are specified. The\n   293\t**projection path** converts the vector to loadable datoms deterministically\n   294\t— segment entity eid `0`, instruction of pc `p` eid `(inc p)`, each tuple\n   295\temitted with `:yin.code/segment 0` and its saturated operands as attribute\n   296\tdatoms (so the round trip vector → datoms → loader is the identity), each\n   297\tresolved-pc operand emitted as a ref to that eid — and hands the batch to\n   298\tthe ordinary §2.6 loader, so every well-formedness check runs on the way in.\n   299\tThe **direct path** decodes the canonical vector into an image without\n   300\tdatoms, as a foreign engine would. The two paths are a conformance\n   301\tobligation: both must yield the same image, and a test in the harness (§7.11)\n   302\tasserts it for every corpus segment. The reference path is the projection;\n   303\tnothing may depend on which path a conforming engine takes.\n   304\t\n   305\tOn lowering, the resumer resolves each address in this order and stops at the\n   306\tfirst hit:\n   307\t\n   308\t1. its own code index for the value's stamp, keyed by address, each entry\n   309\t   verified once by hashing and grammar-validating its vector;\n   310\t2. `:yin.k/carried` in the value itself, verified the same way before load;\n   311\t3. a `dao.jing` read by address, verified the same way.\n   312\t\n   313\tVerification is two checks, always both: the vector must hash to the address\n   314\tit claims (`:yin.k/hash-mismatch` otherwise), and it must satisfy the stamp's\n   315\ttuple grammar — mnemonic set, arity, operand kinds, saturations in place\n   316\t(`:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc otherwise; a correct hash is not\n   317\tstructural validation, the same rule the value decoder applies, §7.5.1). A\n   318\tmiss is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the address (§7.6.5); nothing from a\n   319\tvalue that fails either check is loaded.\n   320\t\n   321\t**The address index is additive; local-id immutability stands.** The §3.1\n   322\trule — an id already holding a different image is a load error, an identical\n   323\treload accepted — is unchanged by UCF, and the first draft's claim that a\n   324\thash check *replaces* it was wrong: two different, correctly hashed segments\n   325\tcan claim the same local id, and redirecting an existing id would silently\n   326\trewire every frame that names it. A lowering engine therefore keeps two\n   327\tmaps: `:code {local-id image}` under the §3.1 rule, and a separate\n   328\t`{stamp {address image}}` index with an `address → local-id` alias column.\n   329\tSegments loaded *by address* may mint fresh local ids; they may never reuse a\n   330\tlive one, and the alias column is checked so one address never lands under\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
