{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '330,690p'\"","aggregated_output":"   330\tlive one, and the alias column is checked so one address never lands under\n   331\ttwo local ids in one VM.\n   332\t\n   333\t**The continuation's own identity and the occurrence.** `:yin.k/id` is the\n   334\t`dao.jing` segment address of the UCF map with `:yin.k/id` removed — and by\n   335\tthe same no-alias argument, the payload published under `:yin.k/id` is the\n   336\t**id-less body**; a reader reconstructs the envelope with\n   337\t`(assoc body :yin.k/id (dao.jing/segment-key body))`. Equal continuations\n   338\tconverge; an idempotent re-publication of one encoding is a no-op. But\n   339\tencodings legitimately differ (§7.5.3), so `:yin.k/id` is *not* what custody,\n   340\tchains, or grants name. They name `:yin.k/occurrence` — the checkpoint\n   341\toccurrence id, minted exactly once, when a park is first exported, unique\n   342\tunder the emitter's attribution, and carried unchanged by every authorized\n   343\tsnapshot variant and every retry of the same park (§7.7.2). A re-emission\n   344\tafter a failed publication reuses the occurrence; it never mints a second\n   345\trunnable checkpoint.\n   346\t\n   347\t## §7.4 Safepoints — where a canonical configuration exists (blocker 1)\n   348\t\n   349\t### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift, and the table is the machine's\n   350\t\n   351\tThe linear machine's registers are canonical only *between* instructions.\n   352\tInside a `:call` that has popped its operands but not yet pushed a frame, or\n   353\tinside a superinstruction the loader fused (§6.4), no ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ exists\n   354\tthat both describes the state and is expressible in the §2.4 vocabulary.\n   355\tUCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be\n   356\tlifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the\n   357\treference machine at which the machine parks. The first draft's table\n   358\tpresented itself as complete while omitting the parks that arrive through\n   359\t`:call` — a called host function that returns an effect blocks exactly as an\n   360\tinstruction effect does, through `apply-call` → `handle-effect` →\n   361\t`call-park-entries`, and both ordinary and tail calls reach that path\n   362\t(`yin.vm.semantic`, `apply-call`). The complete set:\n   363\t\n   364\t| Safepoint kind | Raised by | Canonical resume pc | Stack in frame (St) | Accumulator on resume |\n   365\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   366\t| explicit park | `:park` | `pc+1` | St at park | the resume value |\n   367\t| blocked read | `:stream-next` → `blocked` | `pc+1` | St (cursor ref was in `val`) | the read value; `nil` on `end`, `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\n   368\t| blocked write | `:stream-put` → `full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped target ref | the written value, on retry `ok` |\n   369\t| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/ok` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | the correlated response's `ok` value; its `error` raises |\n   370\t| FFI call, retained | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | as sent, once the request is appended and the response correlates |\n   371\t| effectful call | `:call`/`:tailcall` whose operator yields a blocking effect (`require`, stream-module functions, effect handlers) | `pc+1` | St minus the popped operator and args | the effect's resume value, per its kind as above |\n   372\t| halt | `:halt`, `:return` with empty K | — | — | not resumable; a `:yin.k/type :yin.k/result` travels instead |\n   373\t\n   374\tTwo corrections to the first draft's table stand beside the addition.\n   375\t`:current-continuation` is **not** a safepoint: it does not park — it\n   376\treifies `val ← {seg, pc+1, E, St, K}` and continues, so a reified\n   377\tcontinuation is a *value class* of the encoding (§7.5.1), never a lift\n   378\tpoint. And `pc+1` as the resume pc is verified sound for every row above:\n   379\t§2.6 requires the final pc to be a terminator, so every listed instruction\n   380\thas a successor in its own segment.\n   381\t\n   382\tThe `:yin.k/frame` is exactly the wait-entry shape of `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n   383\t§3.5, with segments replaced by addresses and values by their portable\n   384\tencodings (§7.5):\n   385\t\n   386\t```clojure\n   387\t:yin.k/frame\n   388\t{:yin.k/segment  :segment/sha256-3f1a…\n   389\t :yin.k/pc       5                         ; the resume pc, already pc+1\n   390\t :yin.k/reason   :next                     ; :park | :next | :put | :ffi | :ffi-request | :call-effect\n   391\t :yin.k/val      nil                       ; accumulator; nil except where §7.4.3 pre-fills it\n   392\t :yin.k/stack    [ … ]                     ; St per the table above, encoded\n   393\t :yin.k/env      { sym → encoded value }   ; E\n   394\t :yin.k/k        [ {:yin.k/frame-type :return                    ; or :eval-call / :request-sent,\n   395\t                    :yin.k/segment :segment/sha256-…             ; which also carry :yin.k/call-id\n   396\t                    :yin.k/pc 5 :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/stack-base 0} … ]   ; K, innermost last\n   397\t :yin.k/pending  {…}}                      ; the wait it was in, §7.4.3 — one variant per reason\n   398\t```\n   399\t\n   400\t**Quiescence.** The migration unit is one task: the whole blocked machine\n   401\t(§7.6.3). Lifting additionally requires the machine's ready-queue to be\n   402\tempty — a machine with runnable work queued is mid-scheduling, its next\n   403\tconfiguration is not the parked one, and lift answers `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n   404\t\n   405\t### 7.4.2 Safepoint maps for foreign engines: static facts only\n   406\t\n   407\tThe reference machine needs no metadata because its registers *are* the\n   408\tcanonical configuration. A foreign engine — a register allocator over the\n   409\timage, a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT — keeps its state in physical locations\n   410\tof its own choosing, and the first draft promised it reconstruction data\n   411\tthat is not a function of the code: absolute stack depth and environment\n   412\tkey sets at a pc depend on the *activation* — a closure enters with the\n   413\tcaller's residual operand stack and an environment formed from its captured\n   414\tenvironment plus arguments (`apply-call`), and §2.6 enforces no equal stack\n   415\theights at joins, so the same pc is legitimately reached at many depths.\n   416\tNone of that is derivable, and none of it needs to be: **the frame carries\n   417\tit.** E, St, and every K frame's `:stack-base` and env travel in the value;\n   418\ta foreign engine lowers the canonical frame directly.\n   419\t\n   420\tWhat *is* a function of the canonical vector alone, and what such an engine\n   421\tmust publish — **per segment and per engine profile**, derived from the\n   422\tcanonical stream at its own load time — is the static half:\n   423\t\n   424\t```clojure\n   425\t[[sp :yin.safepoint/segment      :segment/sha256-3f1a…]  ; the code, by address\n   426\t [sp :yin.safepoint/engine       :wasm32/v1]             ; the engine profile that produced this layout\n   427\t [sp :yin.safepoint/pc           5]                      ; canonical resume pc\n   428\t [sp :yin.safepoint/stack-effect -1]                     ; Δ|St| across the parking instruction (static)\n   429\t [sp :yin.safepoint/lexically-required [x conn]]         ; names this pc can read from E (static)\n   430\t [sp :yin.safepoint/layout       {…}]]                   ; engine-owned: physical slot → canonical slot\n   431\t```\n   432\t\n   433\tRules:\n   434\t\n   435\t- `stack-effect` and `lexically-required` are **derived from the canonical\n   436\t  vector alone**, by the same static walk the linearizer performs (§5.3).\n   437\t  They are identical for every engine and checkable against the reference\n   438\t  machine by a conformance test that runs the segment to each safepoint and\n   439\t  compares the observed deltas and E-reads. Absolute depth, activation\n   440\t  bases, captured environments, and physical layouts are **not** claimed,\n   441\t  not derived, and not in this table; they are the frame's business.\n   442\t- `:yin.safepoint/layout` is opaque to the protocol. It is a memo the engine\n   443\t  keeps so that its `lift` is a table lookup. It never travels in a UCF\n   444\t  value; a resumer that is a different engine has its own.\n   445\t- `lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and\n   446\t  `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on safepoints and\n   447\t  undefined elsewhere. The conformance obligation is `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n   448\t  canonical frames, tested against the reference machine's own frames —\n   449\t  which is possible precisely because the dynamic half rides in the frame.\n   450\t- A request to lift at a pc that is not a safepoint of the segment returns\n   451\t  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc. An engine that wants finer\n   452\t  interruption granularity must lower the program to a segment that\n   453\t  *contains* a `:park` where it wants one: that is a different instruction\n   454\t  record, a different address, and a different profile. An engine may not\n   455\t  invent safepoints a record does not have, and two engines that disagree\n   456\t  about the safepoint set of one address are not both conforming.\n   457\t\n   458\tSafepoints are datoms, so a query can ask \"where can this program be\n   459\tmigrated?\" without running it, and a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of\n   460\tthe safepoint set by construction.\n   461\t\n   462\t### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\n   463\t\n   464\tA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\n   465\tthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\n   466\tthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\n   467\t*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot. The first draft carried sketches;\n   468\tthe set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\n   469\teverything a *foreign* resumer needs — including the state the first draft\n   470\tleft implicit on the emitter's heap:\n   471\t\n   472\t```clojure\n   473\t;; blocked read (:stream-next)\n   474\t{:yin.k/reason   :next\n   475\t :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\n   476\t\n   477\t;; blocked write (:stream-put, or an effectful call's :stream/put)\n   478\t{:yin.k/reason   :put\n   479\t :yin.k/stream   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; the target\n   480\t :yin.k/value    <encoded>}            ; the retained value — a retry appends THIS, and\n   481\t                                       ; without it the wait is not re-establishable\n   482\t\n   483\t;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\n   484\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\n   485\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\n   486\t :yin.k/op         :op/add\n   487\t :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\n   488\t :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\n   489\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\n   490\t\n   491\t;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\n   492\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\n   493\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\n   494\t :yin.k/request-op :op/add\n   495\t :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\n   496\t :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\n   497\t :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\n   498\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\n   499\t```\n   500\t\n   501\tThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\n   502\t\n   503\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n   504\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\n   505\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\n   506\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n   507\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\n   508\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n   509\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\n   510\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n   511\t  and Gaps*). This requires the response transport to accept a kept cursor\n   512\t  from another host, which `dao.stream.md` leaves transport-owned and TBD;\n   513\t  UCF therefore declares, per stream, a **portable cursor profile** in\n   514\t  `:yin.k/requires` (`:yin.k/cursor-profiles`), and a transport that does\n   515\t  not declare one cannot be a pending-response endpoint — the lift refuses\n   516\t  it as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before the value is ever minted.\n   517\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\n   518\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\n   519\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\n   520\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\n   521\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\n   522\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\n   523\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\n   524\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\n   525\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\n   526\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\n   527\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\n   528\t- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\n   529\t  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\n   530\t  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\n   531\t  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\n   532\t  on `ok` (`dao.runtime`, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\n   533\t  value); a `:next` wait polls its cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\n   534\t  retained value is recomputed — what parks is what resumes.\n   535\t\n   536\t## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\n   537\t\n   538\t### 7.5.1 A disjoint tagged grammar\n   539\t\n   540\tEncoding is one total function `encode : value × path → outcome`, applied\n   541\trecursively to every value reachable from the frame: accumulator, operand\n   542\tstack, environment values, every frame in K, parked records, and the store\n   543\tslice of §7.6.2. The outcome is either the portable form or a refusal naming\n   544\tthe path.\n   545\t\n   546\tThe first draft encoded markers as ordinary maps — `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`,\n   547\t`{:yin.k/ref addr}` — while passing program map literals through in their\n   548\tnatural shape. That is ambiguous to everyone: §2.5 admits map literals,\n   549\tso a program can *contain* `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`, and a decoder cannot tell\n   550\tan encoder's marker from a program's data. The grammar here is disjoint by\n   551\tconstruction: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**, and\n   552\ta program's own maps are always wrapped.\n   553\t\n   554\t| Encoded form | Tag | Contents | Decodes to |\n   555\t|---|---|---|---|\n   556\t| scalars: nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | — | themselves (never a map; cannot forge a marker) | themselves |\n   557\t| vector, list, set | — | same shape, elements recursively encoded (never a map) | same shape, elements decoded |\n   558\t| literal map | `:yin.k/literal` | `:yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ k₂ v₂ …]`, keys and values recursively encoded | the map, keys and values decoded |\n   559\t| closure | `:yin.k/closure` | `:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}` | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) |\n   560\t| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\n   561\t| parked continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame plus `:yin.k/parked-id` | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` |\n   562\t| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\n   563\t| stream reference | `:yin.k/stream` | `:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …` | an attached handle, under a fresh store key |\n   564\t| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cell, §7.5.3 |\n   565\t| shared subvalue | `:yin.k/ref` | `:yin.k/id addr` | the table entry at `addr`, §7.5.3 |\n   566\t| anything else | — | — | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\n   567\t\n   568\tWhy the wrapping is total rather than an \"escaping rule\" for suspicious\n   569\tshapes: a literal map is wrapped *wherever it occurs and whatever it\n   570\tcontains* — including one that happens to read `{:yin.k/tag …}` — and the\n   571\tentries inside a wrapper are themselves encoded, so a forged marker inside a\n   572\tliteral is re-wrapped one level down and peeled back exactly one level on\n   573\tdecode. No rule asks whether a map \"looks like\" data; no decoder decision\n   574\tdepends on program content. Scalar and collection values pass through only\n   575\tbecause no marker is ever a non-map, which the closed tag table fixes.\n   576\t\n   577\tMap keys are values and are encoded as such — inside `:yin.k/entries`\n   578\talternately with their values, and nowhere else; the frame's own `:yin.k/env`\n   579\tkeys are the program's symbols and are structural, not encoded values.\n   580\t\n   581\t**Decoding validates, independent of hashing.** A hash check confirms what\n   582\twas encoded; it does not confirm the encoding is well formed. The decoder\n   583\twalks the grammar and rejects, as `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the path: a map\n   584\twith no `:yin.k/tag` at an encoded-value position (impossible from a\n   585\tconforming encoder); a tag outside the closed set; a marker missing its\n   586\trequired keys; a `:yin.k/ref` whose address is absent from the value table; a\n   587\tref graph that is cyclic *through ref data* (content-addressed table entries\n   588\tcannot mint such a cycle — a cycle can only be written by hand, and it is\n   589\trejected); and a value table entry not referenced by anything reachable.\n   590\tMalformed-but-correctly-hashed input fails here, before any lowering.\n   591\t\n   592\t### 7.5.2 Primitives: a name is a binding, not a meaning\n   593\t\n   594\t§1.1 admits host functions into the environment because `:var` resolves a\n   595\tprimitive symbol to the function value and a program may then bind it\n   596\t(`(def f +)`). At lift time the emitter recognizes such a value by reverse\n   597\tlookup in its own `:primitives` map — identity on the function object. The\n   598\tfirst draft treated the recovered symbol as the whole story. It is not:\n   599\t\n   600\t- one function object may sit under **several names**, making reverse lookup\n   601\t  ambiguous;\n   602\t- two composition-supplied primitive maps may bind the **same symbol to\n   603\t  different functions** — name presence at the resumer proves nothing about\n   604\t  behavior;\n   605\t- identity stability says nothing about **purity or captured host state**.\n   606\t\n   607\tUCF therefore binds every portable function to a **primitive profile**,\n   608\tpublished data, and the binding is checked on both ends:\n   609\t\n   610\t```clojure\n   611\t;; in :yin.k/requires — computed, not hand-written (§7.6.1)\n   612\t:yin.k/primitives {+\n   613\t                   {:yin.k/profile :yin.k.pp/sha256-…   ; content address of the profile record\n   614\t                    :yin.k/class    :pure               ; :pure | :effectful | :host\n   615\t                    :yin.k/arities  [2]\n   616\t                    :yin.k/effects  #{}                 ; for :effectful — declared effect kinds\n   617\t                    :yin.k/host-state :none}}           ; :none, or refused\n   618\t```\n   619\t\n   620\tThe profile record — `{name class arities effects host-state}` under a\n   621\tcontent address — is what a name must be *checked against*, not merely\n   622\tpresent as. Rules:\n   623\t\n   624\t- **Ambiguity refuses the lift.** A function object that reverse-lookups to\n   625\t  more than one name in the emitter's `:primitives` is refused\n   626\t  (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:ambiguous-primitive`) unless the\n   627\t  composition declares a canonical-name table resolving it to exactly one.\n   628\t- **A host function under no name is refused** (kind `:unnamed-function`) —\n   629\t  a bridge handler's return value, a module-constructed function: it has no\n   630\t  declared semantics to travel under.\n   631\t- **`:host`-class functions are refused** (kind `:host-state-primitive`). A\n   632\t  function whose behavior depends on undeclared host state is not a value\n   633\t  any other host can honor; profiles that declare host state may not be\n   634\t  lifted, and a profile claiming `:host-state :none` is a publication's\n   635\t  warranty, versioned with the contract stamp's primitive-profile registry.\n   636\t- **On lowering, the resumer's binding is checked by profile, not by\n   637\t  presence.** For each required name, the resumer must hold a primitive\n   638\t  whose profile address is equal; a same-named function of different profile\n   639\t  is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the symbol and the expected profile — it is\n   640\t  a routing decision for whoever chose this resumer, not a silent\n   641\t  substitution.\n   642\t- The standard map `yin.vm/primitives` must be published with profiles\n   643\t  (note `yin/def` is `:effectful` with effect `:vm/store-put`, and `require`\n   644\t  is `:effectful` with `:module/require`); that publication is an acceptance\n   645\t  blocker (§7.11).\n   646\t\n   647\t### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\n   648\t\n   649\t**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\n   650\tcaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\n   651\talso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\n   652\tduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\n   653\t\n   654\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\n   655\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\n   656\t  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\n   657\t  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\n   658\t  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\n   659\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\n   660\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\n   661\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\n   662\t  for correctness of sharing.\n   663\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\n   664\t  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\n   665\t  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\n   666\t  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\n   667\t  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\n   668\t  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\n   669\t  draft's two competing ones.\n   670\t\n   671\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\n   672\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\n   673\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\n   674\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\n   675\ta single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\n   676\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\n   677\t\n   678\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\n   679\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\n   680\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\n   681\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\n   682\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\n   683\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n   684\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\n   685\texactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\n   686\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n   687\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n   688\t\n   689\t```clojure\n   690\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '690,1040p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '690,1040p'\"","aggregated_output":"   690\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n   691\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n   692\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\n   693\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\n   694\t```\n   695\t\n   696\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\n   697\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\n   698\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\n   699\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\n   700\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\n   701\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\n   702\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\n   703\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n   704\t\n   705\tA stream reference is portable iff its transport produces a descriptor whose\n   706\t`attach!` can succeed somewhere other than the emitter's host. The encoder\n   707\tcannot know that; it encodes the descriptor and lets the resumer's `attach!`\n   708\treport `not-found` as data. What the encoder *can* refuse is a handle whose\n   709\t`descriptor` call fails, which cannot happen under the contract, and a raw\n   710\tin-memory handle admitted by an in-process stream's admission declaration\n   711\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Creation and Attachment*), which it treats as a host\n   712\tobject.\n   713\t\n   714\t### 7.5.4 Failure is total and names its place\n   715\t\n   716\tEncoding a continuation with one refused leaf refuses the whole\n   717\tcontinuation:\n   718\t\n   719\t```clojure\n   720\t{:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable\n   721\t :yin.k/path   [:yin.k/env 'conn]         ; where in the frame\n   722\t :yin.k/kind   :host-object               ; the closed kind set below\n   723\t :yin.k/hint   \"java.net.Socket\"}         ; classified, never the object\n   724\t```\n   725\t\n   726\tThe kind set is closed:\n   727\t`:host-object | :unnamed-function | :ambiguous-primitive | :host-state-primitive\n   728\t| :cyclic | :in-memory-handle | :non-canonicalizable | :foreign-parked-ref`\n   729\t(the last two from §7.3.2 and §7.6.3). There is no partial UCF value and no\n   730\tplaceholder for a missing leaf. A program that wishes to be migratable keeps\n   731\thost resources behind stream descriptors and named, profiled primitives,\n   732\twhich is the same discipline the host boundary rules already impose on\n   733\tportable code (`datom.world.md`, *Host Boundaries*). The refusal is the\n   734\temitter's outcome, returned as data to whatever asked it to lift; the\n   735\tmachine itself is unaffected and remains parked locally.\n   736\t\n   737\t## §7.6 Dependency closure and context (blocker 3)\n   738\t\n   739\t### 7.6.1 Declared, not hand-written — and discovered conservatively\n   740\t\n   741\tA resumer must be able to decide *before lowering* whether it can run the\n   742\tvalue. `:yin.k/requires` carries that decision's inputs, and every set in it\n   743\tis **computed by a conservative fixed point**, not hand-written and not\n   744\tguessed from the current environment:\n   745\t\n   746\t```clojure\n   747\t:yin.k/requires\n   748\t{:yin.k/segments        #{:segment/sha256-3f1a… :segment/sha256-9c02…}  ; every address in frame, k, closures, values, parked\n   749\t :yin.k/primitives      {sym → profile, §7.5.2}                        ; every {:yin.k/primitive …} encoded, plus every free :var\n   750\t :yin.k/modules         {my.mod :segment/sha256-…}                     ; module name → manifest address\n   751\t :yin.k/effects         #{:stream/next :stream/put :module/require}    ; effect kinds the segments can raise\n   752\t :yin.k/ffi-ops         #{:op/add}                                     ; :yin.code/ffi-op values present\n   753\t :yin.k/streams         #{<identity> …}                                ; logical-stream identities referenced anywhere\n   754\t :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}                         ; transports that must accept kept cursors, §7.4.3\n   755\t :yin.k/discovery       :complete}                                     ; :complete | :incomplete | :blocked — computed, §7.6.5\n   756\t```\n   757\t\n   758\tThe fixed point starts from the frame, its K frames, the parked records of\n   759\t§7.6.3, the store slice, and the pending wait, and repeats until nothing new\n   760\tis reachable:\n   761\t\n   762\t- **Values:** every encoded closure contributes its segment address and its\n   763\t  captured env's values; every stream and cursor marker contributes a stream\n   764\t  identity; every `:yin.k/primitive` marker contributes a name.\n   765\t- **Code:** every reachable segment's instructions are walked — as datoms\n   766\t  where the emitter holds the batch, as canonical tuples where only a vector\n   767\t  was fetched (§7.3.4) — including **`:store-get` and `:store-put`\n   768\t  operands**, which name store\n   769\t  keys directly and which the first draft missed entirely; every `:var`\n   770\t  name; every effect op (`:stream-*`, `:ffi-call`, and the effects of\n   771\t  `:effectful`-class primitives the walk finds); every `:resume` operand\n   772\t  (a parked id, §7.6.3). Walking a *newly fetched* segment continues the\n   773\t  fixed point with its own contents.\n   774\t- **Names:** a `:var` name is satisfied if it is bound in any environment\n   775\t  the value carries, any store key the slice includes, a required primitive,\n   776\t  or a required module. The walk collects *every* `:var` name in reachable\n   777\t  code, not merely those unbound in the top frame's E — E is per-activation\n   778\t  and closures capture different ones, so resolution against \"the current\n   779\t  environment\" is exactly the guess this computation exists not to make. An\n   780\t  unsatisfied name after the fixpoint is a required primitive (checked by\n   781\t  profile, §7.5.2) or a `:yin.k/unsatisfied` entry.\n   782\t- **Modules:** a module's manifest declares its exported primitive profiles\n   783\t  and its **store footprint** (store keys and effect kinds its handlers may\n   784\t  touch). Until manifests carry footprints, any required module whose\n   785\t  footprint is undeclared sets `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` — the honest\n   786\t  statement that closure could not be computed, never a silent\n   787\t  under-approximation. A module effect handler may read store keys the\n   788\t  static walk cannot see; a composition may supply extra slice keys\n   789\t  explicitly, and the value records that it did.\n   790\t\n   791\tBecause the computation is over data, the same query answers \"what does this\n   792\tprogram need?\" for a segment that has never run. And because discovery can\n   793\t*fail to terminate successfully* — a missing segment cannot be walked, an\n   794\tundeclared footprint cannot be closed — the result distinguishes\n   795\t`:complete` from `:incomplete` from `:blocked` (§7.6.5); a fixed point that\n   796\tcould not finish is reported as such, not passed off as a satisfied closure.\n   797\t\n   798\t### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\n   799\t\n   800\tThe store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI\n   801\tpair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A\n   802\tcontinuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key the fixed\n   803\tpoint of §7.6.1 can name — every `:var` name in reachable code, every\n   804\t`:store-get`/`:store-put` operand, every stream/cursor cell — with values\n   805\tencoded by §7.5:\n   806\t\n   807\t```clojure\n   808\t:yin.k/store {counter 41\n   809\t              'log {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}}\n   810\t```\n   811\t\n   812\tThe FFI pair is never in the slice — a program that names the pair's store\n   813\tkeys directly holds handles, and the lift refuses it (§7.5.4). A resumer has\n   814\tits own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.\n   815\t\n   816\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\n   817\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\n   818\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\n   819\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\n   820\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\n   821\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\n   822\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\n   823\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\n   824\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\n   825\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\n   826\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\n   827\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\n   828\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\n   829\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\n   830\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \"Unreachable store entries do not travel\"\n   831\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\n   832\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\n   833\taccident.\n   834\t\n   835\t### 7.6.3 The migration unit is one task\n   836\t\n   837\tThe reference machine's scheduler state does not live in the store, and the\n   838\tfirst draft transported none of it. Both omissions are defects: the\n   839\tmachine's fresh identifiers come from `:id-counter`, and `:resume` resolves\n   840\tits operand against the `:parked` map — a fresh receiver could reuse an\n   841\texisting generated identifier, or fail to resolve a parked continuation a\n   842\tmigrated segment is about to name (`engine/park-continuation`,\n   843\t`engine/resume-continuation`, §3.5). UCF therefore states the unit and\n   844\tcarries the state:\n   845\t\n   846\t- **The unit is the whole blocked machine**: registers (the frame), the\n   847\t  wait-set (as pending variants, §7.4.3), the referenced `:parked` records,\n   848\t  and the fresh-name counter. The ready-queue must be empty at lift\n   849\t  (`:yin.k/not-quiescent` otherwise, §7.4.1) — a task with queued runnable\n   850\t  work has a next configuration that is not the parked one.\n   851\t- **Fresh-name state travels.** `:yin.k/scheduler {:yin.k/id-counter n\n   852\t  …}` carries the counter verbatim; the lowered machine adopts\n   853\t  `max(local, carried)` so generated ids never repeat, and ids already\n   854\t  minted (gensym results in env, stack, or store) travel as ordinary values,\n   855\t  unaffected by the counter.\n   856\t- **Referenced parked records travel or the lift refuses.** The fixed point\n   857\t  of §7.6.1 collects every `:resume` operand in reachable code; each must\n   858\t  resolve within this task — to a parked record carried under\n   859\t  `:yin.k/scheduler :yin.k/parked {pid → frame}` — or the lift is refused\n   860\t  (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:foreign-parked-ref`). Parked records not\n   861\t  named by reachable code do not travel; a record named by *another* task's\n   862\t  code is another task's state and is never silently seized.\n   863\t\n   864\t### 7.6.4 Modules\n   865\t\n   866\t`:yin.k/modules` names each module by the content address of its published\n   867\tmanifest, on the model of `dao.space.index/publish-index!` (`dao.jing.md`,\n   868\t*Publication from an agent*). A module is satisfied when the resumer holds a\n   869\tmodule registry entry (`yin.vm.module` — a value, supplied by the\n   870\tcomposition) whose manifest address is equal; it is not satisfied by a\n   871\tsame-named module of different content. Module *code* is segments and is\n   872\tcovered by `:yin.k/segments` and the manifest's own addressing; the manifest\n   873\tpins the effect handlers, the exported primitive profiles (§7.5.2), and —\n   874\tper §7.6.1 — the store footprint the fixed point needs to claim\n   875\t`:complete`.\n   876\t\n   877\t### 7.6.5 The satisfaction check\n   878\t\n   879\tBefore lowering, the resumer evaluates `:yin.k/requires` against what it\n   880\tholds and answers with one outcome:\n   881\t\n   882\t```clojure\n   883\t{:yin.k/status    :yin.k/unsatisfied\n   884\t :yin.k/discovery :incomplete                    ; or :blocked, §7.6.1 — the computed status of the closure itself\n   885\t :yin.k/missing   {:yin.k/segments       #{:segment/sha256-9c02…}\n   886\t                   :yin.k/primitives     {println <expected profile>}\n   887\t                   :yin.k/modules        {my.mod …}\n   888\t                   :yin.k/streams        #{<identity>}\n   889\t                   :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}}}  ; keys present only where something is missing\n   890\t```\n   891\t\n   892\tThe check reports everything missing in one pass *that the fixed point could\n   893\tsee*, and says which kind of pass it was: `:complete` (the closure is fully\n   894\tcomputed; the missing list is exhaustive), `:incomplete` (a module footprint\n   895\twas undeclared; the missing list is a lower bound), or `:blocked` (a missing\n   896\tsegment stopped discovery; satisfying `:yin.k/segments` and re-running the\n   897\tcheck is the only way forward). A missing segment may be resolved by\n   898\t`dao.jing` fetch (§7.3.4); a missing primitive, module, or cursor profile\n   899\tcannot be resolved by the resumer and is a routing decision for whoever\n   900\tchose this resumer. A resumer never lowers a partially satisfied value; a\n   901\thost with no implementation for something answers with the qualified\n   902\tunsupported outcome, which `datom.world.md` calls a correct outcome, not a\n   903\tgap to be filled.\n   904\t\n   905\t## §7.7 Ownership arbitration — custody as a lease over datoms (blocker 4)\n   906\t\n   907\t### 7.7.1 The race, named\n   908\t\n   909\tThe emitter parks, lifts, appends the UCF value to a medium, and keeps its\n   910\tlocal `:parked` record. Three readers see the value. The emitter's own wait\n   911\tset wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines\n   912\tbelieve they may continue one computation. Nothing in DaoStream can stop\n   913\tthem: a stream has no privileged reader, there is no destructive read, and\n   914\t\"the hard part of a take was never the removal but the *exclusion* of\n   915\tcompeting takers, which is a lease over datoms\" (`dao.stream.md`,\n   916\t*Explicitly Absent*).\n   917\t\n   918\tUCF adopts that sentence as its design. **Resumption is a take. A take is a\n   919\twrite. Exclusion is a lease.** Nothing new is added to the stream contract,\n   920\tand no key is added to any result map.\n   921\t\n   922\t### 7.7.2 Custody facts, and their dispatch keys\n   923\t\n   924\tCustody is negotiated on an **arbitration medium** — a `dao.space` whose\n   925\tfacts are ordinary datoms — using `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged,\n   926\twith the checkpoint **occurrence** as the leased subject. The UCF-authored\n   927\tfacts are dispatch-keyed exactly as lease facts are (`dao.lease.md`,\n   928\t*Vocabulary*: a reader switches on `:dao.lease/status`; a fact carrying it\n   929\tis a lease fact, one carrying neither is ignored):\n   930\t\n   931\t| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Required keys |\n   932\t|---|---|---|---|\n   933\t| offer | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/offered` | emitter | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/id` (this snapshot variant), `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/medium` (the carrier identity) |\n   934\t| checkpoint done | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/resumed` | holder | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/result` — the address of the successor's occurrence value or of a `:yin.k/result` |\n   935\t\n   936\tLease facts themselves keep their own table verbatim — proposal\n   937\t(`:dao.lease/proposed`, with `:dao.lease/subject = {:yin.k/occurrence O}`),\n   938\tgrant (`:dao.lease/accepted`, with `:dao.lease/lease :dao.lease/holder\n   939\t:dao.lease/subject :dao.lease/duration`, and `:dao.lease/max` optional),\n   940\trefusal, release, renewal, lapse — each dispatched on `:dao.lease/status`\n   941\tor `:dao.lease/event`, each with `dao.lease.md`'s required keys, unchanged.\n   942\tA lease judge reading the same medium switches on the lease keys and\n   943\tignores the `:yin.k/custody` facts; a UCF reader switches on\n   944\t`:yin.k/custody` and reads lease facts for authority. Both `:yin.k/custody`\n   945\tfacts are **evidence, not authority**: an offer creates no claim, and a\n   946\t`:resumed` record is a holder's report — completion is the grantor's ledger\n   947\ttransition, not the report (§7.7.6). Authority is exactly `dao.lease.md`'s:\n   948\t**only grantor-authored facts establish terms.**\n   949\t\n   950\tThe subject of every grant is the **occurrence**, never `:yin.k/id`:\n   951\tsnapshot variants and retries of one park share one occurrence, so no pair\n   952\tof encodings can hold independent grants, and an equal-content copy minted\n   953\tby coincidence is a different occurrence and a different computation\n   954\t(§7.3.4).\n   955\t\n   956\t### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\n   957\t\n   958\t`dao.lease.md` is explicit that a judge must be composed **inside the\n   959\tboundary that possesses the resource** and must reclaim *by acting on what\n   960\tit itself holds*; a judge that possesses nothing is outside the contract.\n   961\tThe first draft designated \"the composition-designated arbiter for the\n   962\tcarrier medium,\" defaulting to the emitter — an arbiter relative to a\n   963\tcarrier, possessing an offer, which is a copy of content: the same\n   964\toccurrence carried on a second medium would acquire a second arbiter, and\n   965\tneither arbiter controls execution of anything. This section replaces that\n   966\twith a grounded design.\n   967\t\n   968\t**The possessed resource is the occurrence's execution ledger**: the set of\n   969\tdatoms about one occurrence id on the arbitration `dao.space` — its offer,\n   970\tgrants, renewals, lapses, and resumed reports, plus the occurrence's\n   971\t**admission epoch** (§7.7.5). The **grantor is the boundary that transacts\n   972\tthat space** — its transactor. Possession is exact and of the right kind:\n   973\t\n   974\t- the ledger's datom base lives inside the transactor's boundary, and\n   975\t  admission to it is the transactor's alone — no other boundary can write\n   976\t  those datoms, which is why grantor-authored facts are worth more than\n   977\t  holder reports;\n   978\t- the **reclaim** is the grantor acting on what it holds: recording the\n   979\t  lapse on its own base and advancing the occurrence's epoch datom. It\n   980\t  needs no cooperation from any holder and no reach into any carrier;\n   981\t- the arbiter is **not medium-relative**: the binding\n   982\t  occurrence → arbitration space is minted at first export and travels *in\n   983\t  the value* (`:yin.k/arbitration`, §7.2), so every carrier, every snapshot\n   984\t  variant, and every copy names the same one authority. The same content on\n   985\t  two media does not create a second arbiter, because the authority is named\n   986\t  by the occurrence, not discovered from wherever a value happened to land;\n   987\t- **one holder per occurrence is the grantor's policy, enforced where\n   988\t  policy lives**: `dao.lease` does not impose it, and UCF does not add to\n   989\t  the vocabulary — the grantor's ledger admits at most one live lease per\n   990\t  occurrence (a second grant requires the prior lease reclaimed first,\n   991\t  which `dao.lease.md`'s own validity rules make the grantor the sole\n   992\t  author of). Exclusive custody is thus a property of the ledger the\n   993\t  grantor possesses, not an aspiration over streams it does not.\n   994\t\n   995\tWhat is possessed, concretely, is the *right to admit effects as this\n   996\toccurrence* — the ledger is where epochs and dedup-stamped admissions are\n   997\trecorded, which is what §7.7.5's fencing consults. A composition that\n   998\tcannot wire a `dao.space` transactor as authority cannot offer\n   999\t`:yin.k/exclusive` custody at all; it composes `:yin.k/fork` only. That is\n  1000\tan honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over.\n  1001\t\n  1002\t### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\n  1003\t\n  1004\tEmitter-as-candidate was the first draft's whole answer to the\n  1005\tsource-wakeup race, and it does not close the publication window. The\n  1006\tlifecycle published first and made the emitter a candidate afterward, but\n  1007\tnothing ever removed the emitter's *local* eligibility: its driver may\n  1008\tstill poll the wait set — and polling a blocked writer **performs the\n  1009\tappend** (`dao.runtime`, a `:put` entry retries `append!` on every\n  1010\tpoll) — may restore a woken entry from the ready queue, may satisfy a\n  1011\tdirect `:resume`, and may retry a blocked write, all after the value is in\n  1012\tother machines' hands. Delaying register restoration is too late; the\n  1013\tappend is the effect.\n  1014\t\n  1015\tUCF therefore defines an explicit **exporting** transition that precedes\n  1016\tany publication, and the lift driver performs it as one step over the\n  1017\tmachine value:\n  1018\t\n  1019\t1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\n  1020\t2. **Detach the wait set**: every wait entry moves from the machine's\n  1021\t   wait-set into the export record. The machine's wait-set is now empty,\n  1022\t   so *there is nothing to poll* — no poll, no append, no effect. The\n  1023\t   entries are not lost: the export record retains them verbatim; they are\n  1024\t   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\n  1025\t3. **Neutralize control**: the record's control and K are already absent\n  1026\t   (the machine is parked or blocked); direct `:resume` resolves against\n  1027\t   `:parked`, and the parked records referenced by reachable code travel\n  1028\t   inside the export record — a local `:resume` against them is refused\n  1029\t   while exporting (the restore function observes the exporting machine\n  1030\t   and declines), because the parked id now names a checkpoint that may be\n  1031\t   granted elsewhere.\n  1032\t4. **Publish, then offer**: append segments and the value body; record\n  1033\t   `:yin.k/offered` on the arbitration medium. Only now do readers exist,\n  1034\t   and the source was fenced before any of them did.\n  1035\t\n  1036\tThe exporting machine is pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained\n  1037\texport record — no engine change, no callback, no timer; the drivers of the\n  1038\tworld observe the state as they observe `ready-for-ingress?` (§3.1), and a\n  1039\tdriver that polls an exporting machine's wait set anyway is a host assembly\n  1040\tdefect of the same kind as wiring a refusing deposit destination.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1040,1340p'; tail -90 collab/1789388110000-architect-ucf-revision-r2.glm-5-3.stdout.log\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1040,1340p'; tail -90 collab/1789388110000-architect-ucf-revision-r2.glm-5-3.stdout.log\"","aggregated_output":"  1040\tdefect of the same kind as wiring a refusing deposit destination.\n  1041\t\n  1042\t**Failure recovery and idempotent retry.** If publication fails — a full\n  1043\tcarrier, an unreachable arbitration medium — the machine stays exporting\n  1044\tand the composition retries; a retry re-encodes or reuses the encoded value\n  1045\tand **reuses the occurrence** (§7.3.4), so no retry mints a second runnable\n  1046\tcheckpoint. Abandoning the export reinstates the export record into the\n  1047\tmachine (wait entries return to the wait-set, parked records to `:parked`)\n  1048\tand the task resumes as a purely local one — the sole paths back to local\n  1049\texecution are *abort export* (before the offer is recorded) and *a grant to\n  1050\tthe emitter itself* (after); there is no third.\n  1051\t\n  1052\t### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\n  1053\t\n  1054\t`dao.lease.md` states it plainly: **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is\n  1055\tnever evidence of tenure.** The first draft's fencing — stamp effects with\n  1056\tthe lease id, let consumers consult the medium and drop effects whose\n  1057\tincarnation has a `:lapsed` record — fails in every direction the review\n  1058\tnamed: a partitioned consumer accepts stale effects (it cannot see the\n  1059\tlapse); a delayed legitimate effect is discarded after release; repeated\n  1060\teffects within one lease share one incarnation and are indistinguishable; a\n  1061\treplacement holder repeats an effect its predecessor committed; and apply\n  1062\tids correlate requests with responses, they do not suppress duplicates.\n  1063\tLapse-record filtering is correlation, not fencing. What UCF requires\n  1064\tinstead:\n  1065\t\n  1066\t**Authority epochs, checked atomically with commitment.** The occurrence's\n  1067\tledger carries an epoch datom, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim\n  1068\t(§7.7.3) — a monotone number the possessing boundary alone can write. A\n  1069\tconsumer that must be exactly-once (a `dao.space` transactor, an FFI callee\n  1070\twith side effects) admits a fenced effect only in the same act that reads\n  1071\tthe epoch: the check and the commit are one transition against the\n  1072\tadmission resource, never check-then-act. An effect whose incarnation's\n  1073\tepoch is not the current one is refused at commitment. This is\n  1074\t`dao.lease.md`'s \"fencing from the resource\" for durable resources, and it\n  1075\tis a composition duty that composition owes exactly once.\n  1076\t\n  1077\t**Stable operation ids, and durable dedup.** Every fenced effect carries\n  1078\t`:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}` — assigned by the fenced\n  1079\twriter in emission order. Op-ids are deterministic functions of the\n  1080\tcheckpoint and the operation sequence: a replacement holder resuming the\n  1081\t*same* occurrence replays the same op-ids, which is what makes\n  1082\tacross-incarnation dedup possible at all. Exactly-once consumers keep a\n  1083\tdurable `{op-id → committed result}` record, written in the same atomic\n  1084\ttransition as the epoch check; a replayed op-id returns the recorded result\n  1085\tand commits nothing. Within one incarnation and across incarnations, the\n  1086\tsame op-id is the same logical operation — the room for exactly-once\n  1087\tsemantics that lease identity alone cannot provide.\n  1088\t\n  1089\t**Where the stamp rides.** Program values are program data; UCF never\n  1090\tedits them. A resumed incarnation running under a lease emits through a\n  1091\t**fenced writer** the composition wires: a writer that wraps each appended\n  1092\tvalue in an envelope `{:yin.k/envelope … :yin.k/incarnation <lease>\n  1093\t:yin.k/op-id … :yin.k/value <the program value, verbatim>}`. Exactly-once\n  1094\tconsumers read stamped envelopes; a stream wired bare — no fence — is\n  1095\t*declared* unprotected, and a composition that needs exactly-once delivery\n  1096\tthrough it has wired the wrong thing. Fencing is opt-in per stream, exactly\n  1097\tas `dao.lease.md` places the burden: \"a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n  1098\tfrom the resource.\" No callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\n  1099\tits own lease bound (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*), and what it did past\n  1100\tthe bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\n  1101\t\n  1102\t**Durable completion of a checkpoint.** Publication of a successor is not\n  1103\tcompletion. The holder's exit sequence is: append the successor value to\n  1104\tthe carrier, append `:yin.k/resumed` (evidence) to the arbitration medium,\n  1105\tappend `:dao.lease/released`. Completion is the **grantor's ledger\n  1106\ttransition** — observing the release (or the `:resumed` evidence followed\n  1107\tby release) and closing the occurrence's tenure. The crash windows are then\n  1108\tbounded and harmless: a crash after the successor append but before the\n  1109\trecords leaves an orphan successor — content-addressed, idempotent,\n  1110\tre-readable as evidence but granting nothing; the lease lapses by silence;\n  1111\tthe grantor re-grants the *last recorded* occurrence, and the orphan chain\n  1112\tis queryable history (§7.7.6). A crash mid-sequence never requires the\n  1113\tgrantor to trust a holder's report as proof of durable completion, because\n  1114\tthe report is evidence and the ledger is authority.\n  1115\t\n  1116\t**Partition policy, stated honestly.** A fenced consumer that cannot reach\n  1117\tthe authority (epoch unreadable) must **suspend protected effect\n  1118\tcommitment** — fail closed — or the composition has chosen at-least-once\n  1119\tdelivery with after-the-fact dedup reconciliation; which one is a declared\n  1120\tproperty of the consumer, and a consumer that neither checks nor declares\n  1121\tis not exactly-once and may not claim to be. A holder partitioned from the\n  1122\tarbitration medium runs to its own lease bound and its effects are\n  1123\tafter-the-fact distinguishable, which is the guarantee `dao.lease.md`\n  1124\tactually makes; UCF does not promise more. A grantor that lost its ledger\n  1125\treclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`, *Restart*); **permanent** authority\n  1126\tloss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\n  1127\tand repair is a governance act outside this protocol. The arbitration\n  1128\tmedium's durability is therefore a composition duty owed before\n  1129\t`:yin.k/exclusive` is offered at all (`dao.lease.md`, *Composition\n  1130\tduties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\n  1131\t\n  1132\t### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\n  1133\t\n  1134\tA resumed continuation runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\n  1135\tagain. Either way it produces a successor value — a `:yin.k/continuation`\n  1136\tor a `:yin.k/result` — whose `:yin.k/origin` carries the predecessor's\n  1137\t`:yin.k/occurrence` and the lease it ran under. Each park is a **new\n  1138\toccurrence**: minted fresh by the running holder, chained to its\n  1139\tpredecessor, and itself the subject of the next custody cycle. The holder\n  1140\tappends the successor, records `:yin.k/resumed` with the successor's\n  1141\taddress, and releases. The chain of occurrences linked by `:yin.k/origin`\n  1142\tis the migratory computation's history, queryable on the arbitration\n  1143\tmedium: where it ran, under which grant, what it became. A rollback is a\n  1144\tre-offer of an earlier link; effects past that link need compensation, not\n  1145\trecomputation (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4) — and §7.7.5's op-ids\n  1146\tare what compensation reads to find what was done.\n  1147\t\n  1148\t### 7.7.7 Restart\n  1149\t\n  1150\tA grantor that lost its ledger reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1151\t*Restart*: reclaim first, then grant afresh — a new grant alone does not\n  1152\tend the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\n  1153\toccurrence ledgers it transacts; its recoverable holder for each is the\n  1154\t`:dao.lease/holder` of the last grant, both of which are facts on (or the\n  1155\trecoverable state of) the space it possesses. Both are queries, which is\n  1156\twhy custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier stream.\n  1157\t\n  1158\t## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n  1159\t\n  1160\t**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n  1161\t\n  1162\t1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\n  1163\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  1164\t2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n  1165\t   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\n  1166\t   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\n  1167\t3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n  1168\t   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\n  1169\t   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n  1170\t   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n  1171\t   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n  1172\t   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\n  1173\t4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\n  1174\t   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n  1175\t   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\n  1176\t   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\n  1177\t   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\n  1178\t5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\n  1179\t   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\n  1180\t   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\n  1181\t   data.\n  1182\t\n  1183\t**Lower** (resumer side):\n  1184\t\n  1185\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n  1186\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n  1187\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\n  1188\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\n  1189\t   both checks always run.\n  1190\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\n  1191\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\n  1192\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\n  1193\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\n  1194\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\n  1195\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n  1196\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\n  1197\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\n  1198\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1199\t   *Authority*).\n  1200\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\n  1201\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n  1202\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\n  1203\t   cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\n  1204\t   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n  1205\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\n  1206\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\n  1207\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\n  1208\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\n  1209\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\n  1210\t8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\n  1211\t   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\n  1212\t   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\n  1213\t9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\n  1214\t   (§7.7.6), record `:yin.k/resumed`, release.\n  1215\t\n  1216\tNeither sequence contains a callback. Each is a step function a\n  1217\tcomposition-supplied driver repeats, as for every other interpreter in the\n  1218\tsystem.\n  1219\t\n  1220\t## §7.9 Outcome algebra\n  1221\t\n  1222\tEvery UCF operation returns one map dispatching on `:yin.k/status`; the set\n  1223\tis closed and every non-`ok` outcome carries the data needed to act on it.\n  1224\t\n  1225\t| `:yin.k/status` | Raised by | Carries |\n  1226\t|---|---|---|\n  1227\t| `:yin.k/ok` | any | the value, or the lowered VM |\n  1228\t| `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` | lift | `:yin.k/segment`, `:yin.k/pc` |\n  1229\t| `:yin.k/not-quiescent` | lift | the ready-queue depth |\n  1230\t| `:yin.k/non-portable` | lift | `:yin.k/path`, `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/hint` — kinds at §7.5.4, cycles included as `:kind :cyclic` |\n  1231\t| `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` | lower | `:yin.k/contract` expected and found |\n  1232\t| `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` | lower | the address claimed and the address computed |\n  1233\t| `:yin.k/undecodable` | lower | `:yin.k/path`, the structural defect — malformed markers, missing or cyclic refs, malformed-but-correctly-hashed bodies |\n  1234\t| `:yin.k/unsatisfied` | lower | `:yin.k/missing`, `:yin.k/discovery` — includes attachment failures, cursor profiles, and an unreachable arbitration medium |\n  1235\t| `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the proposal — a step status while a proposal is unanswered, bounded by nothing (`dao.lease.md`) |\n  1236\t| `:yin.k/not-holder` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the lease state observed |\n  1237\t\n  1238\tTerminal DaoStream outcomes met during lift or lower (`transport-error`,\n  1239\t`closed`, `invalid-descriptor`, …) are surfaced unchanged inside the UCF\n  1240\toutcome under their own key `:dao.stream/outcome`, exactly as the transport\n  1241\tproduced them; UCF does not rename them, nest them under private keys, or\n  1242\ttranslate between them. FFI requests and responses inside a value use the\n  1243\treference machine's versioned envelope, `dao.stream.apply` —\n  1244\t`:dao.stream.apply/id`, `/op`, `/args`, `/ok`, `/error` — never the\n  1245\tdeleted v1 `dao.stream.apply` vocabulary. Every lower failure names its\n  1246\tcleanup obligation: before custody, none; after custody, release\n  1247\t(§7.8 step 7).\n  1248\t\n  1249\t## §7.10 Invariant compliance\n  1250\t\n  1251\t| Invariant | How UCF honors it |\n  1252\t|---|---|\n  1253\t| No hidden global state | Every dependency is declared in `:yin.k/requires` — *as computed by the declared fixed point, with its completeness stated* (`:yin.k/discovery`); the store travels as an explicit slice restored into an isolated store; fresh-name state and parked records travel explicitly; custody is facts on a named, possessed medium. |\n  1254\t| No implicit control flow | Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine's parking transitions define; the successor chain is explicit `:yin.k/origin` links; the exporting state is an observed data state, never a notification. |\n  1255\t| No callbacks | Lift and lower are step functions; custody is observed, never notified; a lapsed holder stops at its own bound; an unanswered proposal is a step status, not a wait. |\n  1256\t| No shared mutable state | A UCF value is content-addressed and immutable; occurrence identity separates content variants from the one leased computation; two resumers of one value under `:fork` diverge into distinct incarnations; the isolated execution store shares nothing with the resumer's tasks. |\n  1257\t| No layer collapsing | Canonicalize, encode, discover, arbitrate, load, restore, execute are separate functions with data between them. The engine's hot loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the blocked machine's value, and the exporting state is that value with an emptied wait-set and a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery. |\n  1258\t| No assumed graphs | Sharing is an explicit value table keyed by address; cursor aliasing is an explicit cell graph, not inferred from positions; code refs are pcs relative to an addressed vector; nothing is resolved by pointer; foreign engines reconstruct dynamic state from the frame, never from layouts they assume. |\n  1259\t\n  1260\tAnd the axioms — each claim conditioned on the obligation it depends on: the\n  1261\tcontinuation is a stream value (1); its meaning is supplied by the\n  1262\tresumer's loader and contract stamp, not carried in it (2); its code and its\n  1263\tstate are datoms under `:yin.code/*` and `:yin.k/*` (3); and it pauses,\n  1264\ttravels, and resumes anywhere that **holds what it declares, satisfies the\n  1265\tdeclared cursor profiles, and obtains custody where the policy demands it**\n  1266\t(4) — the unconditional form of that sentence was a claim the unresolved\n  1267\tstate closure, custody enforcement, and effect admission had not earned.\n  1268\t\n  1269\t## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\n  1270\t\n  1271\tThe warning at the top of this file is retained until each of these is\n  1272\tclosed with a test in the parity or conformance suite. The first four are\n  1273\tthe review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\n  1274\t*accepting the design*, not merely work items.\n  1275\t\n  1276\t- **Authority grounding and wiring.** The arbitration `dao.space`, its\n  1277\t  transactor as grantor, the occurrence ledger with epochs, and the\n  1278\t  attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` requires, are composition duties; the\n  1279\t  first composition to ship UCF is `yin.repl.core`'s handoff demo, and\n  1280\t  its wiring becomes the worked example. A composition without a\n  1281\t  transactable space offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n  1282\t- **Execution identity.** Occurrence minting, the `:yin.k/origin` chain,\n  1283\t  retry idempotence (one occurrence per park), and successor completion as\n  1284\t  a grantor ledger transition need conformance tests, including the crash\n  1285\t  windows of §7.7.5.\n  1286\t- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\n  1287\t  round-trip and resume *elsewhere*: retained writes, sent and retained FFI\n  1288\t  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\n  1289\t  through the aliasing scenarios of `check-wait-set`. This depends on a\n  1290\t  **portable cursor profile** in at least one transport, which\n  1291\t  `dao.stream.md` leaves TBD — an external dependency UCF inherits and\n  1292\t  cannot close itself.\n  1293\t- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\n  1294\t  and durable op-id dedup at consumers: which consumers check, and how a\n  1295\t  `dao.space` transactor consults the epoch without a callback, is a design\n  1296\t  item for `dao.space.transactor.md`. Until it lands, `:yin.k/exclusive`\n  1297\t  custody is at-least-once and must say so.\n  1298\t- **Canonical byte encoding.** `:yin.code/hash` and `:yin.k/id` are exactly\n  1299\t  as portable as `dao.jing/segment-key`, which is transitional. Closing this\n  1300\t  is `dao.jing`'s open item, not UCF's, but UCF cannot be declared\n  1301\t  cross-host stable before it lands.\n  1302\t- **Contract revision publication.** `:yin.code/contract \"v2\"` needs a\n  1303\t  published revision history naming the complete execution contract\n  1304\t  (§7.3.3): the tuple grammar — mnemonic set, per-mnemonic arity and operand\n  1305\t  kinds, saturation/defaults table — the opcode table and transitions, the\n  1306\t  resolution and last-value-wins rules, the effect outcome map, and the\n  1307\t  scheduler semantics, written into `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 beside the\n  1308\t  opcode table.\n  1309\t- **Primitive profile publication.** The standard `yin.vm/primitives`\n  1310\t  map published with profiles (§7.5.2), including the `:effectful`\n  1311\t  declarations for `yin/def` and `require`; reverse-lookup uniqueness\n  1312\t  asserted at `create-vm`.\n  1313\t- **Discovery completeness.** The fixed point of §7.6.1 implemented and\n  1314\t  tested against the `:store-get`/`:store-put`, dynamic-callee, and\n  1315\t  cross-activation-`E` cases; module manifests publishing store footprints\n  1316\t  so `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` disappears.\n  1317\t- **Isolated-store lowering.** Conformance tests that the resumed task's\n  1318\t  store contains exactly the slice and the resumer's pair, and that a\n  1319\t  receiver-side binding of a name in the slice's name set cannot change\n  1320\t  resolution.\n  1321\t- **Safepoint conformance harness.** A test that runs every corpus segment\n  1322\t  on the reference machine to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table —\n  1323\t  effect-producing calls included — lifts, lowers into a fresh VM, and\n  1324\t  checks the parity suite's result; the same harness is what a foreign\n  1325\t  engine runs to claim conformance, including `lift ∘ lower = id`, the\n  1326\t  static `stack-effect`/`lexically-required` checks, and the §7.3.4\n  1327\t  projection/direct-path image-equality assertion.\n  1328\t- **Exporting-state integration.** The lift driver's exporting transition\n  1329\t  and its failure/retry paths, tested specifically against the\n  1330\t  poll-a-blocked-writer-appends hazard of §7.7.4.\n  1331\t\n[claude-code:unrecognized_model] {\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"query_source\":\"sdk\"}\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-14 12:54:55 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-14 19:54:55 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh (+07)\nCoding-Agent: glm\nSession-ID: ee3147ec-24c9-4fd7-8578-395b3282ab53\n\n**Deliverable:** `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` revised in full (684 → 1309 lines), plus the one-line `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.2 sync (`:yin.code/hash` type `string` → `segment address`, with the pre-existing uncommitted §7 pointer edit preserved). Nothing staged or committed.\n\n**Finding-by-finding resolution** (all 19 accepted; none rejected):\n\n1. **P1 Canonicalization not total / local-identity-dependent — fixed in §7.3.2.** The flat `[e a v]` triple sort is gone entirely, replaced by a schema-bearing **canonical instruction record** (nested map: segment header + `{pc → {attr → value}}`). Admissible attribute set per entity kind (§2.4 operand table only; anything else refuses as `:non-canonicalizable`); `:yin.code/hash` itself, provenance, eids, `t`/`m` excluded; `:yin.code/segment` normalized away structurally. No cross-type comparison exists anywhere — the record is ordered only by the `dao.jing` encoder's key-printed normalization (`order-normalize`, verified to sort by `pr-str`, never `compare` across types). Byte-codec pinning stays an explicit dependency (§7.11).\n\n2. **P1 Sort collision of `1,2` vs `2,1` — fixed in §7.3.2** by canonicalizing **the loader's resolved interpretation** (last-value-wins applied first, same as `index-batch` in `code.cljc:46`/`semantic.cljc:512`). The two batches resolve to indexed values `2` and `1`, execute differently, and now address differently. The resolution rule is versioned with the contract stamp. \"Execute identically ⟺ address identically\" narrowed to: equal iff resolved interpretations are equal — no equivalence over pc layouts, gensym prefixes, or equivalent sequences.\n\n3. **P1 Addresses don't address stored payloads — fixed in §7.3.4.** The payload materialized at a code address **is the canonical record**; raw batches address differently and are explicitly not a resolution mechanism (`:yin.k/carried` carries records, not batches). Record→batch conversion specified deterministically (eids `0`/`(inc p)`, pc operands back to refs) through the ordinary §2.6 loader. Continuation payload is the **id-less body**; envelope reconstructed via `(assoc body :yin.k/id (segment-key body))`.\n\n4. **P2 Stamp scope + id-immutability — fixed in §7.3.3/§7.3.4.** The stamp now versions the **complete execution contract**: schema/well-formedness/resolution, opcode table and transitions, `resolve-var` precedence, effect→outcome mapping, call restoration and scheduler semantics. Code indices are per-stamp (`{stamp {address image}}`). The draft's \"hash check replaces the §3.1 policy\" claim retracted: local-id immutability stands; the address index is additive with an alias column. `:yin.code/hash` type synced in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.2.\n\n5. **P1 Missing `:call`-effect safepoints — fixed in §7.4.1.** The table gains the effectful-call row (`:call`/`:tailcall` yielding a blocking effect, via `apply-call`→`handle-effect`→`call-park-entries`) plus post-pop stack shapes for every row, and the two FFI variants split. Also corrected beyond the findings: `:current-continuation` removed from the table — it reifies a value and continues; it is a value class (§7.5.1), not a park.\n\n6. **P1 Stack depth/env keys not static — fixed in §7.4.2.** Static (derived from the record alone, published per engine): `stack-effect` and `lexically-required`. Dynamic (absolute depth, activation bases, captured envs): declared **underivable** and carried in the frame — the foreign engine lowers the canonical frame directly. An engine inserting `:park` produces a new record/address/profile; it cannot add safepoints to the old address.\n\n7. **P1 Tag collision with literal maps — fixed in §7.5.1.** Disjoint tagged grammar: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**; program maps are always wrapped as `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/literal :yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ …]}` with keys and values recursively encoded (so a forged `{:yin.k/tag …}` is re-wrapped one level down and peeled exactly once). Decode-time validation is total and hash-independent: unknown tags, missing keys, missing/unused value-table refs, ref-cycles → `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n8. **P1 Primitive identity ≠ semantics — fixed in §7.5.2.** Primitive **profiles** (content-addressed; class `:pure`/`:effectful`/`:host`, arities, effect kinds, host-state) travel in `:yin.k/requires`. Ambiguous reverse lookup refuses (`:ambiguous-primitive`) unless a canonical-name table is declared; unnamed functions refuse; `:host`-class refuses; lowering checks **profile equality**, not name presence. Publication of `yin.vm/primitives` profiles is a blocker (§7.11).\n\n9. **P1 Encoding-dependent identity as execution identity — fixed in §7.3.4/§7.7.2.** `:yin.k/id` (content, encoding-dependent, for dedup) separated from `:yin.k/occurrence` (checkpoint occurrence, minted once at first export, stable across variants and retries). Leases subject the occurrence; a retry never mints a second runnable checkpoint.\n\n10. **P1 Discovery omits `:store-get`, no exact closure — fixed in §7.6.1/§7.6.5.** Conservative fixed point over frame, K frames, parked records, store values, pending ops, code (including `:store-get`/`:store-put` operands and *every* `:var` name in reachable code — not just those unbound in the top E), fetched segments, and module manifests with declared footprints. `:yin.k/discovery ∈ {`:complete`, `:incomplete`, `:blocked`}` distinguishes incomplete discovery from a satisfied closure; the satisfaction outcome reports which pass it was.\n\n11. **P1 Store merge changes resolution + contradiction — fixed in §7.6.2/§7.8 step 7.** Both merge sentences deleted; no merge in either order. The resumed task runs in an **isolated execution store**: exactly the slice + remapped cells + the resumer's FFI pair. The receiver's local store never participates, so its `+` binding cannot change resolution; no key can collide because no receiver key exists; resource entries remap to fresh local keys. The FFI pair never travels (naming its keys directly refuses the lift).\n\n12. **P1 Scheduler state missing — fixed in §7.6.3/§7.4.1.** Migration unit declared: one task — the whole blocked machine, ready-queue empty at lift (else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`). `:yin.k/scheduler` carries the `:id-counter` (adopted as `max(local, carried)`) and every parked record named by a reachable `:resume` operand; a parked reference outside the task refuses (`:foreign-parked-ref`).\n\n13. **P1 Pending operations incomplete — fixed in §7.4.3.** Exhaustive variants with full resumption state: blocked write with retained value + target; sent FFI with correlation id, op, both endpoints, and the response cursor cell at its **kept** position; retained FFI with request fields + both endpoints + position. Response-position hazard addressed (kept cursor, never `:oldest`/`:newest`; whole-task waiters travel so `check-wait-set` round order is preserved). The fixed-local-key restore defect fixed: pending calls route to the **emitter's** pair via carried descriptors and remapped cell keys; the resumer's pair serves only future calls. A **portable cursor profile** is required per transport and declared in `:yin.k/requires` (the `dao.stream.md` TBD is named an external dependency, §7.11).\n\n14. **P1 Cursor aliasing erased — fixed in §7.5.3.** **Cursor cells**: logical cell ids mapped to `{stream, position}` in `:yin.k/cells`; values reference cells (`:yin.k/cursor-ref`), never positions. Cells are deliberately *not* content-addressed — deduplicating by content is exactly the aliasing erasure. Two cells at one position stay independently advanceable; shared cells keep shared advancement (`check-wait-set`'s observable semantics); lowering remaps cells to fresh store entries.\n\n15. **P1 Grantor authority ungrounded — fixed in §7.7.3.** The possessed resource is the **occurrence's execution ledger** — the datoms about one occurrence on the arbitration `dao.space` — and the grantor is **the boundary that transacts that space**. Reclaim = the grantor acting on its own base (lapse record + epoch advance); the occurrence→authority binding is minted at first export and travels *in the value* (`:yin.k/arbitration`), so the arbiter is not medium-relative; one-holder-per-occurrence is grantor ledger policy where `dao.lease.md` puts policy. Without a transactable space, `:exclusive` is honestly unavailable (fork only).\n\n16. **P1 Publication/wakeup window — fixed in §7.7.4 (and §7.8 lift, §7.10).** An explicit **exporting** transition precedes any publication: ready-queue empty, wait-set detached into the export record (so there is nothing to poll — no poll, no append, no effect), direct `:resume` declined. Failure recovery and idempotent retry specified (same occurrence reused); abort-export and grant-to-self are the sole paths back to local execution. §7.10's claim updated: hot loop untouched, exporting is driver-visible data state — no longer \"execution machinery untouched.\"\n\n17. **P1 Lapse-filtering ≠ fencing — fixed in §7.7.5.** Authority **epochs** checked atomically with effect commitment (never check-then-act); deterministic **op-ids** `{:occurrence, :seq}` with durable `{op-id → result}` dedup at exactly-once consumers (covering within-incarnation, post-release, and replacement-holder repeats); fenced writers wrap envelopes outside the program value (stamping bare program values would corrupt them — a refinement of the draft's own flawed mechanism); durable completion defined as the grantor's ledger transition with crash windows enumerated; partition policy fail-closed or declared at-least-once; grantor loss via `dao.lease` restart, permanent loss stated as irreparable. Honest weakening recorded; §7.11 keeps enforceable fencing as a blocker.\n\n18. **P2 Vocabulary incomplete/inconsistent — fixed in §7.4.3, §7.7.2, §7.9, §7.2.** FFI envelopes use `dao.stream.apply` keys (`:dao.stream.apply/id` etc.); nested stream outcomes preserved under `:dao.stream/outcome` (not `:dao.stream/status`); the outcome algebra completed (`:undecodable` for malformed-but-correctly-hashed, `:not-quiescent`, `:awaiting-grant`, unsatisfied-with-discovery, cleanup rule for every post-custody failure); cycle refusal collapsed to one representation (`:non-portable` + `:kind :cyclic`); `:yin.k/result` schema defined (§7.2); the custody fact table carries its dispatch key and required keys, lease facts keep theirs verbatim.\n\n19. **P3 Compliance overclaims — fixed in intro, §7.10, §7.11.** The §7.10/§7.11 cross-reference corrected; every compliance claim conditioned on its unresolved obligation (discovery completeness, custody enforcement, effect admission); \"step loop untouched\" narrowed to the true claim; the fourth axiom's \"resumes anywhere that holds what it declares\" conditioned on cursor profiles and custody. §7.11 retitled **Acceptance blockers**, with authority grounding, execution identity, pending completeness, and enforceable fencing as its first four items.\n\nOne note for the orchestrator's re-weigh: two fixes go beyond the reviewers' suggested mechanisms where I judged the defect real but the fix imprecise — the tag grammar wraps *every* literal map rather than escaping only suspicious shapes (context-free totality), and effect fencing stamps a wrapper envelope rather than adding keys to program values (adding `:yin.k/incarnation` to a program value would be program-visible mutation). The `:current-continuation` safepoint-row removal (§7.4.1) is a table-completeness correction implied by but not enumerated in finding 5.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
