{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'primitive|store-get|store-put|gensym|identical|vector\\\\?|list\\\\?' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md | head -180; nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '97,121p;137,158p;172,220p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | sed -n '45,71p'\"","aggregated_output":"2:  \"Canonical data model (Datoms), schema, and primitives for the Yin Abstract\n10:     `:make-stream` constructor exactly as it hands it `:primitives`. There is\n100:(def primitives\n127:   ;; Definition primitive - returns an effect\n128:   'yin/def (fn [k v] {:effect :vm/store-put, :key k, :val v}),\n129:   ;; Module loading primitive - returns an effect dispatched to the host\n131:              (let [ns-sym (if (vector? spec) (first spec) spec)]\n226:   :gensym 9,\n227:   :store-get 10,\n228:   :store-put 11,\n262:                    :gensym 9,\n263:                    :store-get 10,\n264:                    :store-put 11,\n363:            (cond (and (contains? cardinality-many-attrs a) (vector? v))\n442:                  ;; VM primitives\n443:                  :vm/gensym (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/gensym)\n445:                  :vm/store-get (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/store-get)\n447:                  :vm/store-put (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/store-put)\n468:                  ;; Continuation primitives\n534:       :vm/gensym (assoc base :prefix (get-attr root-id :yin/prefix))\n535:       :vm/store-get (assoc base :key (get-attr root-id :yin/key))\n536:       :vm/store-put (assoc base\n589:                                         (if (vector? v) v [v])))\n620:                                      (if (vector? v) v [v])))\n634:     :primitives   primitive operations map (defaults to `primitives`)\n703:        :primitives (or (:primitives opts) primitives),\n     1\t# dao.space.transactor — The Agent-Side Transactor\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: implemented on `dao.stream` (migrated 2026-09-09; the v1\n     4\t`:transactor` DaoStream type it replaces is deleted). This document is the\n     5\tpermanent record of the transactor's contract: what a transactor is, the\n     6\tinvariants its tests pin, where durability lives, and the one wiring\n     7\trequirement a host composition must honour. The executable contract is\n     8\t`test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc`.\n     9\t\n    10\t**Related documents:**\n    11\t- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — the tuple space; *Three Boundaries* and\n    12\t  *The Write Path* place the transactor in the medium\n    13\t- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — `publish!` delegates here from the\n    14\t  transactor; the snapshot reads this namespace's local stream\n    15\t- `docs/design/dao.space.schema.md` — the validating wrapper that owns a\n    16\t  transactor value\n    17\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` — the storage boundary publication targets\n    18\t- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` and `docs/design/dao.stream.md` — the\n    19\t  contract this namespace consumes\n    20\t\n    21\t## A transactor is a value, not a stream\n    22\t\n    23\tA transactor is an **interpreter over a stream, not a stream**\n    24\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Composition*): it reads via the local stream's reader\n    25\tsurface, appends through its writer surface, and needs no support from the\n    26\tstream contract for either. The v1 `:transactor` DaoStream type — a\n    27\tregistered dispatch record carrying two live handles — could never cross the\n    28\tdescriptor boundary v2 requires, so it was never a descriptor; its options\n    29\tmap is now the constructor's plain **spec**:\n    30\t\n    31\t```clojure\n    32\t(require '[dao.stream.memory-log :as memory-log]\n    33\t         '[dao.space.transactor :as transactor])\n    34\t\n    35\t(def local (:dao.stream/handle\n    36\t            (memory-log/create! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/memory-log})))\n    37\t\n    38\t(def log (transactor/create! {:local-stream local   ; reader+writer surface;\n    39\t                              :intake-pool [intake] ; supplied, never owned\n    40\t                              :name \"worker-7\"}))   ; optional, diagnostic\n    41\t\n    42\t(transactor/append!   log {:db/id 1 :work/claims \"task\"})\n    43\t(transactor/transact! log [{:db/id 1 :work/claims \"task\"} …])\n    44\t(transactor/publish!  log opts)   ; → {:manifest-address … :manifest …}\n    45\t(transactor/close!    log)        ; → {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}\n    46\t;; reads: use `local` — it is the caller's handle and always was\n    47\t```\n    48\t\n    49\tThere is no registry and no `open!` dispatch: `create!` is a plain function,\n    50\tfollowing `query/open-published!`. A transactor implements no dao.stream\n    51\tprotocol at all — it is not on a stream, so it has no identity to project.\n    52\t`next` was pure delegation on a handle the caller still holds, and `closed?`\n    53\tis *Explicitly Absent* in v2 because \"a predicate answer is stale the moment\n    54\tit returns; operation results are authoritative\" — the replacement is in the\n    55\tvocabulary: `append!`/`transact!` on a closed transactor answer\n    56\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` as data.\n    57\t\n    58\t`create!` returns **the value, or throws** — its failure modes are a\n    59\tmalformed spec and a malformed retained history, both of which a caller can\n    60\tonly abort on. It validates **surfaces, never retention**:\n    61\t`stream/reader?`/`stream/writer?` (T16's pool check is the same surface\n    62\trule), and a reader/writer surface check does not establish retention.\n    63\t\n    64\t## The invariants\n    65\t\n    66\tPinned by `test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc` unless noted:\n    67\t\n    68\t| # | Invariant |\n    69\t|---|---|\n    70\t| T1 | Every `append!`/`transact!` writes exactly ONE atomic transaction record `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}` through exactly one local append, so no reader observes a torn transaction |\n    71\t| T2 | `t` comes from a per-value watermark, derived on `create!` as 0 for an empty history else 1 + max datom `t` |\n    72\t| T3 | A caller-supplied `:next-t` is rejected |\n    73\t| T4a | A malformed retained history fails the create |\n    74\t| T5 | The watermark advances **iff** the local append answered `:dao.stream/ok`; a failed or thrown append leaves the same `t` retryable |\n    75\t| T6 | Single-writer: two values over one local stream derive the same `t` and write colliding records; a documented hazard, not coordinated |\n    76\t| T7 | Close is per value: it rejects further writes and neither closes nor erases the local stream or the intake pool |\n    77\t| T8 | Close linearizes after an in-flight append |\n    78\t| T9 | Creating a transactor creates, registers, or closes nothing |\n    79\t| T10 | Entity maps require `:db/id`; an explicit datom `t` is rejected; `[e a v]`/`[e a v nil m]` pad and validate |\n    80\t| T11 | `publish!` passes the local stream, pool and opts to `index/publish-index!` and returns its result |\n    81\t| T15 | An ok receipt carries the outcome, the allocated `t`, and the datoms — `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms ds}` |\n    82\t| T16 | Intake pool members are validated with v2 `stream/writer?`; a non-empty collection is required |\n    83\t| T18 | The local stream is on a transport declaring complete retention (below) |\n    84\t| T19 | `dao.space.schema` installs its next state only when the transactor answered ok (pinned in `schema_test`) |\n    85\t| T20 | Retired 2026-09-09 under schema's plan; schema returns the transactor's receipt unchanged and adopts its closed-precedence rule |\n    86\t\n    87\t## Operational outcomes are data; argument defects throw\n    88\t\n    89\tThe write path validates the local append's answer with\n    90\t`stream/valid-outcome?` before interpreting it, and answers:\n    91\t\n    92\t| Case | v2 behaviour |\n    93\t|---|---|\n    94\t| local append ok | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms ds}`, watermark advances |\n    95\t| local append `full`/`closed`/`invalid-value`/`transport-error` | returned as data, watermark **unchanged**, same `t` still pending |\n    96\t| local append answers a non-outcome | folded to `:dao.stream/transport-error` with `:dao.stream/answer` retained |\n    97\t| transactor itself closed | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` |\n    98\t| malformed entity map / explicit datom `t` / empty `tx-data` / zero datoms | **still throws** — defects in the caller's own argument, detected before any stream is touched |\n    99\t| local append throws | propagates, watermark unchanged |\n   100\t\n   101\t`full` means \"not yet, retry\", so throwing on it was always wrong; the retry\n   102\tcontract is now stronger, not weaker: *the watermark advances if and only if\n   103\tthe local append answered `:dao.stream/ok`*, so retrying the identical call\n   104\tre-attempts the identical `t`. On a `memory-log` local stream the only\n   105\treachable non-ok outcome is `closed`, since the transport excludes `full`,\n   106\t`invalid-value` and `transport-error` — but `append!`/`transact!` are library\n   107\tfunctions over whatever handle they are given, which is why the `full` branch\n   108\tis pinned by a writer double rather than left untested.\n   109\t\n   110\t## T18: the local-log wiring requirement\n   111\t\n   112\t> The host composition supplies `dao.space` a local handle created by\n   113\t> `dao.stream.memory-log/create!`. Its declared complete retention makes\n   114\t> fresh `:oldest` cursors true origin cursors for `derive-next-t` and\n   115\t> `publish-index!`; supplying an evicting transport is a host-assembly defect.\n   116\t\n   117\tA **declaration, not a check**. `create!` does not and must not verify\n   118\tretention, although the misassembly is detectable at least twice over:\n   119\t`stream/descriptor` exposes `:dao.stream/type`, so a type check would catch a\n   120\tring buffer today, and the contract's *Complete history* names the kept\n   121\torigin cursor as a detection mechanism in its own right. Neither is done,\n   122\ton purpose:\n   123\t\n   124\t- **A type check would couple `dao.space` to `memory-log` by name** and\n   125\t  reject a future correct transport — a durable append-only log, a spilling\n   126\t  log — that declares the same complete retention. That is precisely what\n   127\t  \"declared, never interrogated\" exists to prevent, and why `dao.stream.md`\n   128\t  lists a retention predicate as *Explicitly Absent*.\n   129\t- **A kept origin cursor buys nothing here.** On a complete-retention\n   130\t  transport a fresh `:oldest` *is* the origin, and the transactor does not\n   131\t  create the local stream, so it could not mint at origin even if it wanted\n   132\t  to. The mechanism is for consumers on transports that can evict.\n   133\t\n   134\tSo the residual risk is real and accepted deliberately: a wrongly wired\n   135\tevicting transport's surviving suffix would be read and reported as the\n   136\twhole history, silently. A `stream/reader?`/`stream/writer?` surface check\n   137\tdoes not establish retention — the defence is this requirement being written\n   138\twhere someone wiring a composition will read it: here, in\n   139\t`dao.space.index.md` (*The snapshot* / *The agent-transactor loop*), in\n   140\t`dao.space.md` (*The Write Path*), and in ADR 0003's amendment.\n   141\t\n   142\t## Where durability lives\n   143\t\n   144\tThe local stream is the **authoritative record for the logical stream's\n   145\tlifetime** — the watermark and every published index are derived from it —\n   146\tand it is **not durable**: `memory-log` is process-lifetime, and a restarted\n   147\tprocess sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity. That is the\n   148\tcorrect shape, not a shortfall:\n   149\t\n   150\t- **Stage 1, append** — the write lands in the agent's local stream and no\n   151\t  storage handle is touched.\n   152\t- **Stage 2, publish** — the covered indexes and manifest are appended to an\n   153\t  intake stream and a DaoJing observer materializes them into content\n   154\t  storage.\n   155\t\n   156\t**The durable record is what publication puts in `dao.jing`.** Un-published\n   157\twrites are not durable, and never were — v1's local streams were in-memory\n   158\tring buffers, so this was equally true before the migration; the design\n   159\tmerely claimed otherwise. The pipeline mirrors Datomic's memory-index →\n   160\tdisk-index, and a memory index is not durable either. A durable *stream*\n   161\ttransport is not the answer and should not be proposed as the fix: it would\n   162\tput a second durable record beside the content store, which is exactly the\n   163\t`dao.stream`/`dao.jing` unification `dao.space.query.md`'s *Decisions*\n   164\talready ruled out.\n   165\t\n   166\t## Open items\n   167\t\n   168\t- **The O(history) watermark replay.** `create!` derives `t` by reading the\n   169\t  whole retained history (`index/snapshot-datoms`), and `publish!` rebuilds\n   170\t  the indexes from the origin — both correct today *because the transport\n   171\t  cannot evict*. The eventual relief keeps one truth — the log carries its\n   172\t  own checkpoint, from which the watermark and incremental indexes resume —\n   173\t  rather than adding a second stored counter that can disagree with the\n   174\t  record. Not designed here.\n    97\t## The judge\n    98\t\n    99\tThe judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource** and\n   100\treclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\n   101\tresource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\n   102\tinterpreter, which is not the forbidden collapse: it is the possessing host\n   103\tinterpreter performing its own act.\n   104\t\n   105\t**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\n   106\t\n   107\t- its **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;\n   108\t- its **tenure start**, the reading the grant was seeded at, which no renewal\n   109\t  changes;\n   110\t- its **last relevant observation**, the reading of the grant or newest\n   111\t  eligible renewal, whichever is later;\n   112\t- its **evidence state** — `known`, or `unknown` with its resumed reading;\n   113\t- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\n   114\t  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\n   115\t\n   116\tA lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\n   117\tand **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\n   118\tappended with `:dao.stream/ok`. Until both, it remains.\n   119\t\n   120\tThe ledger is not rebuildable from any stream: a composition either persists it,\n   121\tas a stream its owner writes and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.\n   137\t3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\n   138\t   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n   139\t   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n   140\t4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n   141\t   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\n   142\t   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n   143\t   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n   144\t   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n   145\t   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\n   146\t   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n   147\t   `:silence` only once a full duration has passed since its resumed reading.\n   148\t5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n   149\t   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\n   150\t   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n   151\t   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n   152\t6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n   153\t   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n   154\t   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\n   155\t   first clause of step 4.\n   156\t\n   157\tThe record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\n   158\tfact is never evidence of tenure.**\n   172\t### Restart\n   173\t\n   174\tA grantor that has lost its ledger **reclaims and re-grants**: for each\n   175\tresource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first, then grants afresh.\n   176\tThe reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does not.\n   177\t\n   178\tThis requires an inventory of possessed resources and a recoverable holder for\n   179\teach, both of which belong to the resource rather than to this vocabulary.\n   180\tA grantor with neither persists its ledger instead.\n   181\t\n   182\t## The holder\n   183\t\n   184\t- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n   185\t  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n   186\t  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n   187\t- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n   188\t  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n   189\t  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n   190\t- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n   191\t  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n   192\t  whether or not anything has been heard.\n   193\t- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records it.\n   194\t\n   195\tThe bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n   196\tfrom the resource.\n   197\t\n   198\t## Composition duties\n   199\t\n   200\tA composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n   201\t\n   202\t- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n   203\t  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n   204\t- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n   205\t- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n   206\t- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n   207\t  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n   208\t  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n   209\t  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n   210\t  at assembly.\n   211\t- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n   212\t- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n   213\t  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n   214\t- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n   215\t  refused at assembly otherwise.\n   216\t- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n   217\t  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n   218\t  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n   219\t  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n   220\t\n    45\t(defn- order-normalize\n    46\t  \"Normalize a value so equal values print identically: maps sort by printed\n    47\t   key, sets sort by printed element, sequences recurse.\n    48\t\n    49\t   Transitional: this exists only to make the print-based content hash\n    50\t   deterministic and order-insensitive until the pinned, cross-platform\n    51\t   canonical byte encoding lands (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Canonical\n    52\t   encoding). It is NOT that canonical encoding.\"\n    53\t  [v]\n    54\t  (cond (map? v) (->> v\n    55\t                      (map (fn [[k x]]\n    56\t                             [(order-normalize k)\n    57\t                              (order-normalize x)]))\n    58\t                      ;; a pr-str-keyed sorted map prints its keys in a\n    59\t                      ;; fixed order on every platform (array-map is not\n    60\t                      ;; in ClojureDart)\n    61\t                      (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2)))))\n    62\t        (set? v) (list 'set (sort-by pr-str (map order-normalize v)))\n    63\t        (sequential? v) (mapv order-normalize v)\n    64\t        :else v))\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(defn- order-normalized-print\n    68\t  \"Transitional encoder: pr-str over the order-normalized form. NOT the final\n    69\t   canonical byte encoding; see order-normalize.\"\n    70\t  [v]\n    71\t  (pr-str (order-normalize v)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"The revision fixes several concrete problems: addresses now name the stored payloads, duplicate attributes are resolved before hashing, and the destination store is isolated. I’m finding gaps in the new replay and completion rules, though: operation numbers alone do not make replay deterministic, and release currently has conflicting meanings for completion and failed lowering."}}
