{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"Small checks against the reference code confirmed three remaining problems: the current hash encoder gives list and vector literals the same address despite different `conj` behavior; adopting the receiver’s counter changes the next observable `gensym`; and a name bound in one captured environment can still require a primitive in another activation. I’m separating these counterexamples from fixes that are sound but still await implementation."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md | sed -n '64,83p;142,164p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '143,191p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '211,248p;282,322p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '59,85p'; rg -n ':yin.k/|:yin.safepoint/' src --glob '*.cljc'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md | sed -n '64,83p;142,164p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '143,191p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '211,248p;282,322p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '59,85p'; rg -n ':yin.k/|:yin.safepoint/' src --glob '*.cljc'\"","aggregated_output":"    64\t## The invariants\n    65\t\n    66\tPinned by `test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc` unless noted:\n    67\t\n    68\t| # | Invariant |\n    69\t|---|---|\n    70\t| T1 | Every `append!`/`transact!` writes exactly ONE atomic transaction record `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}` through exactly one local append, so no reader observes a torn transaction |\n    71\t| T2 | `t` comes from a per-value watermark, derived on `create!` as 0 for an empty history else 1 + max datom `t` |\n    72\t| T3 | A caller-supplied `:next-t` is rejected |\n    73\t| T4a | A malformed retained history fails the create |\n    74\t| T5 | The watermark advances **iff** the local append answered `:dao.stream/ok`; a failed or thrown append leaves the same `t` retryable |\n    75\t| T6 | Single-writer: two values over one local stream derive the same `t` and write colliding records; a documented hazard, not coordinated |\n    76\t| T7 | Close is per value: it rejects further writes and neither closes nor erases the local stream or the intake pool |\n    77\t| T8 | Close linearizes after an in-flight append |\n    78\t| T9 | Creating a transactor creates, registers, or closes nothing |\n    79\t| T10 | Entity maps require `:db/id`; an explicit datom `t` is rejected; `[e a v]`/`[e a v nil m]` pad and validate |\n    80\t| T11 | `publish!` passes the local stream, pool and opts to `index/publish-index!` and returns its result |\n    81\t| T15 | An ok receipt carries the outcome, the allocated `t`, and the datoms — `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms ds}` |\n    82\t| T16 | Intake pool members are validated with v2 `stream/writer?`; a non-empty collection is required |\n    83\t| T18 | The local stream is on a transport declaring complete retention (below) |\n   142\t## Where durability lives\n   143\t\n   144\tThe local stream is the **authoritative record for the logical stream's\n   145\tlifetime** — the watermark and every published index are derived from it —\n   146\tand it is **not durable**: `memory-log` is process-lifetime, and a restarted\n   147\tprocess sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity. That is the\n   148\tcorrect shape, not a shortfall:\n   149\t\n   150\t- **Stage 1, append** — the write lands in the agent's local stream and no\n   151\t  storage handle is touched.\n   152\t- **Stage 2, publish** — the covered indexes and manifest are appended to an\n   153\t  intake stream and a DaoJing observer materializes them into content\n   154\t  storage.\n   155\t\n   156\t**The durable record is what publication puts in `dao.jing`.** Un-published\n   157\twrites are not durable, and never were — v1's local streams were in-memory\n   158\tring buffers, so this was equally true before the migration; the design\n   159\tmerely claimed otherwise. The pipeline mirrors Datomic's memory-index →\n   160\tdisk-index, and a memory index is not durable either. A durable *stream*\n   161\ttransport is not the answer and should not be proposed as the fix: it would\n   162\tput a second durable record beside the content store, which is exactly the\n   163\t`dao.stream`/`dao.jing` unification `dao.space.query.md`'s *Decisions*\n   164\talready ruled out.\n   143\t## Materialization rule\n   144\t\n   145\tThe materialization is an idempotent union of content-addressed values. If\n   146\t`H(x)` is the address of the canonical encoding of `x`, observing `x` means:\n   147\t\n   148\t```clojure\n   149\t(assoc-if-absent target (H x) x)\n   150\t```\n   151\t\n   152\tRe-observing the same value is a no-op. Observing equal values through\n   153\tdifferent pool streams is also a no-op after the first insertion. Consequently:\n   154\t\n   155\t- replay is safe;\n   156\t- pool streams may be consumed in any interleaving;\n   157\t- duplicate publication is harmless;\n   158\t- immutable content writes do not require cross-stream coordination; and\n   159\t- a materialization can be reconstructed by replaying the available pool\n   160\t  streams.\n   161\t\n   162\tThe implemented write is `dao.jing/materialize!`: it derives the address from\n   163\tthe payload alone (`segment-key`) and asks the backend's `:put-content-fn`\n   164\tfor an explicit verdict. A backend validates before it writes: the address must\n   165\tbe a segment address and must hash to the payload, else it throws and stores\n   166\tnothing. `:inserted` means the value is durably stored now;\n   167\t`:present` means an equal value is already stored there, in which case the\n   168\tstored value is read back and verified. A collision in which an existing\n   169\taddress contains different canonical bytes is an integrity failure, not an\n   170\toverwrite, and throws loudly. `nil` is a legal payload distinct from absence:\n   171\t`get` takes a caller-supplied not-found and returns stored `nil` as `nil`.\n   172\t\n   173\t### Canonical encoding\n   174\t\n   175\tContent addressing is meaningful only when equal supported values produce the\n   176\tsame bytes on every participating platform. Distinct values must address\n   177\tdistinctly, both across types (`42` and `\"42\"` do not collide) and within one\n   178\ttype (`{:a 1}` and `{:a 2}` do not). Minted addresses are readable EDN: the\n   179\tname never starts with a digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`.\n   180\tThe canonical encoder is therefore part of the storage contract even though the\n   181\tmeaning of the encoded value is not.\n   182\t\n   183\tCanonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n   184\tsets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n   185\tsuch as datoms, index orders, manifests, or any notion of a root.\n   186\t\n   187\tThe target encoding is a canonical flat byte representation suitable for\n   188\tcross-platform hashing and in-place reading. The current implementation uses\n   189\tan order-normalized `pr-str` as a transitional encoder — deterministic and\n   190\torder-insensitive, but not yet the pinned canonical byte encoding. This is the\n   191\tfirst open item under *Open items and current limitations*.\n   211\t(defn handle-next\n   212\t  \"Handle :stream/next. Total over the seven read outcomes.\n   213\t\n   214\t   `ok` advances the store cursor to the exact returned successor. `blocked`\n   215\t   parks. `end` yields nil, as v1 did. `gap` advances to the recovery cursor\n   216\t   and yields `:dao.stream/gap`, so a program that reads it learns values were\n   217\t   lost. The three terminal outcomes are errors.\"\n   218\t  [state effect]\n   219\t  (let [cursor-ref (:cursor effect)\n   220\t        cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)\n   221\t        store (:store state)\n   222\t        cursor-data (get store cursor-id)]\n   223\t    (when (nil? cursor-data) (fail \"Invalid cursor reference\" {:ref cursor-ref}))\n   224\t    (let [stream-id (:stream-id cursor-data)\n   225\t          handle (get store stream-id)]\n   226\t      (when (nil? handle)\n   227\t        (fail \"Stream not found for cursor\" {:stream-id stream-id}))\n   228\t      (let [result (stream/next handle (:cursor cursor-data))\n   229\t            o (outcome result)\n   230\t            advance (fn [state* value]\n   231\t                      {:value value,\n   232\t                       :state (assoc state*\n   233\t                                     :store\n   234\t                                     (assoc store\n   235\t                                            cursor-id\n   236\t                                            (assoc cursor-data\n   237\t                                                   :cursor\n   238\t                                                   (:dao.stream/cursor\n   239\t                                                     result))))})]\n   240\t        (case o\n   241\t          :dao.stream/ok (advance state (:dao.stream/value result))\n   242\t          :dao.stream/blocked {:park true,\n   243\t                               :cursor-ref cursor-ref,\n   244\t                               :stream-id stream-id,\n   245\t                               :state state}\n   246\t          :dao.stream/end {:value nil, :state state}\n   247\t          :dao.stream/gap (advance state :dao.stream/gap)\n   248\t          (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n   282\t(defn check-wait-set\n   283\t  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n   284\t\n   285\t   Every entry is resolved to a live handle and an opaque cursor out of the\n   286\t   store before `dao.runtime` polls it — resolution happens per poll, so\n   287\t   the stored entry itself never carries a handle. There is no\n   288\t   transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only mechanism.\n   289\t\n   290\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n   291\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n   292\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values — the\n   293\t   value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next — instead\n   294\t   of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll cursor. An entry\n   295\t   that stays waiting is retained in its stored, resource-id form; the\n   296\t   resolved copy was for this poll only.\"\n   297\t  ([state] (check-wait-set state nil))\n   298\t  ([state restore-fn]\n   299\t   (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n   300\t     (if (empty? wait-set)\n   301\t       state\n   302\t       (loop [remaining wait-set\n   303\t              v (assoc state :wait-set [])\n   304\t              woken []]\n   305\t         (if (empty? remaining)\n   306\t           (let [new-tasks (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken restore-fn)]\n   307\t             (update v :ready-queue (fnil into []) new-tasks))\n   308\t           (let [entry (first remaining)\n   309\t                 augmented (augment-wait-entry (:store v) entry)\n   310\t                 ;; A singleton wait set makes the outcome below belong to\n   311\t                 ;; this entry alone.\n   312\t                 polled (rt/check-wait-set (assoc v\n   313\t                                                  :wait-set [augmented]\n   314\t                                                  :ready-queue []))\n   315\t                 raw (first (:ready-queue polled))\n   316\t                 ;; A woken reader's successor is stored before later entries\n   317\t                 ;; resolve, so a shared cursor-ref advances within the round.\n   318\t                 advance (when (and raw (:cursor-ref raw) (:cursor raw))\n   319\t                           {(:id (:cursor-ref raw))\n   320\t                            (assoc (get (:store polled) (:id (:cursor-ref raw)))\n   321\t                                   :cursor (:cursor raw))})]\n   322\t             (recur (rest remaining)\n    59\t## Authority\n    60\t\n    61\t- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\n    62\t  tenure. A holder fact establishes no term; a delayed holder renewal is still\n    63\t  evidence and counts as such.\n    64\t- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n    65\t  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\n    66\t  measured as though it had not arrived.\n    67\t- **At most one of `:accepted` or `:rejected` answers a proposal.**\n    68\t  Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal identity.\n    69\t- **A proposal creates no state.** Nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor\n    70\t  owes no answer, and a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by\n    71\t  nothing in this vocabulary.\n    72\t- **A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the\n    73\t  holder is the party the grant is delivered to.\n    74\t- **A `:policy` cause may not consist of declining to count evidence\n    75\t  received.** Eligible renewals are counted whether or not another condition\n    76\t  ends the lease.\n    77\t\n    78\t## Prohibitions\n    79\t\n    80\t- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n    81\t  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n    82\t- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\n    83\t- No absolute time appears in any fact.\n    84\t- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n    85\t- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"bb -cp src/cljc /dev/stdin <<'CLJ'\n(require '[yin.vm.semantic :as s] '[yin.vm.code :as c])\n(defn call-batch [tail]\n [[0 :yin.code/type :segment] [0 :yin.code/length 2]\n  [1 :yin.code/segment 0] [1 :yin.code/pc 0] [1 :yin.code/op :call] [1 :yin.code/argc 0] [1 :yin.code/tail? tail]\n  [2 :yin.code/segment 0] [2 :yin.code/pc 1] [2 :yin.code/op :halt]])\n(prn {:probe :tail-coercion :validities (mapv #(c/well-formed? (call-batch %)) [nil false true :yes]) :images (mapv #(-> (s/load-image (call-batch %)) :code first) [nil false true :yes])})\n(def p [[0 :yin.code/type :segment] [0 :yin.code/length 1] [1 :yin.code/segment 0] [1 :yin.code/pc 0] [1 :yin.code/op :jump] [1 :yin.code/target 10]])\n(prn {:probe :shape-versus-control-validation :tuple [[:jump 9]] :projection-defect (c/well-formed? p)})\nCLJ\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
